ralph/progress.txt:407:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, lifecycle-projection-chokepoint instance (BE-0408 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_deployment_lifecycle_e2e_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/deployment_lifecycle_e2e_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestDeploymentLifecycleE2ECoversCallSites + TestDeploymentLifecycleE2EPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Deployment Lifecycle End-to-End Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests (BE-0408)" subsection; THIRTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0407 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestDeploymentLifecycleE2E ./...`, CI step name `Deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests`, verify.sh prefix #32 inserted between BE-0407's #31 and the trailing optionals renumbered #32-#36 → #33-#37 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 36 → # 37` and synthetic-fixture `# 35 → # 36` updated to match, plus BE-0400's trailing-optional commentary `#32-#35 → #33-#36`). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST lifecycle-projection chokepoint (a slog.LogValuer pure projection on a closed-set lifecycle-state-machine row): (1) `slog TEXT AND JSON HANDLERS BOTH EMIT A time= ATTRIBUTE THAT BREAKS DETERMINISTIC ROUND-TRIP ASSERTIONS — INSTALL A ReplaceAttr HOOK THAT DROPS slog.TimeKey AT THE TOP-LEVEL GROUP ONLY (return slog.Attr{} when len(groups)==0 && a.Key==slog.TimeKey)`. Scope the drop to top-level groups so a customer-named attribute called `time` inside the projected group is still asserted on. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer and whose canonical pair asserts repeat-call determinism on the rendered slog form. (2) `LIFECYCLE CHOKEPOINTS (closed-set rows whose terminal-vs-non-terminal enum classification implies a database CHECK on a sibling timestamp column) MUST PIN THE DATABASE-CHECK INVARIANT AT THE GO PROJECTION INSIDE THE CANONICAL PAIR's CLOSED-SET SELF-CHECK SEQUENCE`. The deployments_finished_consistent table CHECK becomes self-check #4 here: non-terminal status (queued, running) ⇒ nil FinishedAt; terminal status (succeeded, failed, cancelled, rolled_back) ⇒ set FinishedAt. Otherwise an in-memory regression that violates the CHECK only surfaces against a live Postgres; targeted canonical-pair gates do not. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a row from a table whose closed-set status column has a sibling-column CHECK constraint. (3) `THE STRUCTURAL WHITELIST/BLACKLIST ASSERTION FOR A slog.LogValuer SHOULD DRIVE THROUGH THE JSON HANDLER (not the text handler) SO A REGRESSION THAT SWITCHED THE HANDLER STILL TRIPS THE CONTRACT`. The redaction contract isn't "this text never contains this string" (defeated by an escape change); it's "this group projects these keys and not these keys". JSON-route the projection, json.Unmarshal into map[string]any, assert keys whitelist + blacklist. The text-handler canary still fires for the secret-marker absence canary; both surfaces should be asserted side-by-side. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer with a documented field whitelist. One canonical-pair-package gotcha worth pinning forward: `WHEN THE CHOKEPOINT'S EXPORTED API LIVES ON A VALUE RECEIVER OF A TYPE DECLARED IN THE PARENT PACKAGE (here store.Deployment with LogValue() on the value receiver), THE CANONICAL PAIR FILE GOES IN package <parent>_test AND IMPORTS THE PARENT PACKAGE — mirrors BE-0379..BE-0407 convention; no white-box `package store` test needed`. The chokepoint is `package store_test`'s view of `(store.Deployment).LogValue()`, with helper renders (`deploymentLifecycleRenderLogValue`, `deploymentLifecycleStructuredLogValue`) wrapping the slog API. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:408:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:410:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, port-driven-pure-classifier instance (BE-0406 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_import_dry_run_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/migrateimport/import_dry_run_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestImportDryRunCoversCallSites + TestImportDryRunPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Import Dry-Run Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: import dry-run tests (BE-0406)" subsection; TWENTY-EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0405 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestImportDryRun ./...`, CI step name `Import dry-run tests`, verify.sh prefix #30 inserted between BE-0405's #29 and the trailing optionals renumbered #30-#34 → #31-#35 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 34 → # 35` and synthetic-fixture `# 33 → # 34` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST port-driven-pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `(*migrateimport.Importer).Plan(ctx, PlanInput) (Plan, error)` which is deterministic but reads from port interfaces (Scanner, Repository) rather than being a package-level pure function: (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH IMPORTER PER ITERATION — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE THE CHOKEPOINT TAKES PORT INTERFACES AT CONSTRUCTION TIME` — unlike BE-0405 (which has no shared receiver because Diff is a package-level function), the import-dry-run chokepoint is a method on Importer whose fake Scanner/Repository are scenario-specific. Each goroutine therefore builds its own (Snapshot, fake Repository, Importer) triple per iteration. The burst's value shifts from "package has no shared state" to "the planner's internal pure logic (classify, pendingOwnerItems, proposeSlug, indexProjectsByID, indexEnvironmentsByID, indexServicesByID) has no package-level shared state" — a regression that smuggled in a cached owner table or a sync.Once mutating a per-action map would still surface as a per-iteration mismatch. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-from-ports classifier (`pkg.New(cfg).Method(args)` style): build a fresh chokepoint per iteration; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's pure helpers have no package-level shared state. (2) `THE DRY-RUN PURITY INVARIANT IS A POSITIVE ASSERTION ON THE FAKE REPOSITORY'S RECORDED CALLS, NOT THE ABSENCE OF AN EFFECT — SCAN repo.calls FOR EVERY CREATE PREFIX AFTER EVERY Plan CALL` — the importDryRunCheckOutcome predicate scans the fakeRepo.calls slice for any string with prefix `CreateProject:`, `CreateEnvironment:`, or `CreateService:` and returns a failure when one is found; pending-owner rows additionally assert len(repo.calls) == 0 (the no-owner short-circuit must not touch the repository at all). A regression that started writing through Plan would fail the prefix-scan on its first ready scenario; a regression that started reading the repository under a pending-owner short-circuit would fail the count assertion on its first pending-owner scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented read-only on some paths and read-write on others: pin the read-only contract as a positive assertion over the fake's call recorder, not as the absence of an observable side-effect (a state diff would silently pass when the planner reads but does not write, which is itself a regression). (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY ASSERTS MARKER-SURVIVAL IN ProposedDisplay AND MARKER-ABSENCE IN STATUS / REASON / LEVEL — NOT IN ProposedSlug, BECAUSE SLUG IS DERIVED FROM DISPLAY` — the value-free classification contract says the importer's classification surface (Reason, Status, slug, display name) is value-free for sensitive content, but ProposedSlug is itself derived from ProposedDisplay via domain.NormalizeSlug and will deliberately carry alphanumeric content from the human-authored name. The canary therefore seeds importsecretmarker into untrusted Dokploy display names and (a) asserts the marker survives in at least one item's ProposedDisplay (operator review surface MUST render the human name), (b) asserts the marker is absent from string(Level), string(Status), and string(Reason) of every item (closed-set tags MUST stay value-free). Asserting on slug too would over-tighten: a slug derived from a name containing the marker WILL carry the marker by design. Watch out for MaxSlugLen=30 truncation: marker-bearing test names MUST stay ≤ 30 chars so the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug matches the planner's normalized slug exactly (a longer name truncates and the dominance test silently flips to StatusReady). Forward-applicable to every verification story whose value-free contract has a deliberate exception field (display, label, raw-name): enumerate the closed-set tag fields the marker MUST be absent from; do not include the deliberate-exception field in the absence predicate; pin marker survival in the exception field too so the canary's positive direction is also pinned. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `domain.ID FIXTURES MUST USE CROCKFORD BASE32 LOWERCASE EXCLUDING i, l, o, u` — the importer's fake repository accepts any string but human-facing IDs in test fixtures follow the canonical Crockford suffix; `proj_01hz00000000000000000000dp` and `proj_01hz00000000000000000000ap` are valid (4 + 20 + 2 = 26 chars), while invented IDs like `proj_…colp` or `proj_…linp` are NOT (they contain `l` or `o`). (B) `NormalizeSlug TRUNCATES TO MaxSlugLen=30 AND TRIMS TRAILING HYPHENS` — keep marker-bearing fixture names short enough that their normalized slug equals their raw input verbatim; the duplicate-collision scenario's pre-seeded slug uses the same shortened name as the snapshot to avoid silent slug-mismatch drift. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:411:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-classifier instance (BE-0405 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_reconciliation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/reconcile/reconcile_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestReconciliationCoversCallSites + TestReconciliationPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Reconciliation Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: reconciliation tests (BE-0405)" subsection; TWENTY-SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0404 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestReconciliation ./...`, CI step name `Reconciliation tests`, verify.sh prefix #29 inserted between BE-0404's #28 and the trailing optionals renumbered #29-#33 → #30-#34 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 33 → # 34` and synthetic-fixture `# 32 → # 33` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `reconcile.Diff(desired, actual) Plan` which is a package-level pure function (no receiver, no I/O, no time, no shared state): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST SHARES NO INSTANCE — EVERY GOROUTINE CALLS THE PACKAGE-LEVEL Diff DIRECTLY` — unlike BE-0395..BE-0398 (which each shared an instance because the chokepoint had observable mutable state) and unlike BE-0404 (which shares a *BreakGlassService), the reconcile burst has NO shared instance: each goroutine calls `reconcile.Diff(desired, actual)` directly with its own scenario-derived inputs. The contention's value is precisely the assertion that the package itself has no shared state — a regression that smuggled in a cached reason table, a sync.Once mutating a per-action map, or a sync.Pool reused without resetting would surface as a per-iteration mismatch even when the aggregate count matched, because every goroutine knows its own predicted closed-set tag. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a package-level pure function (`pkg.Fn(args)` style): skip the shared-instance pattern entirely; the burst's value is the purity assertion on the package. (2) `DETERMINISTIC-ORDERING SELF-CHECK FIRES BEFORE THE TABLE WALK AND BEFORE THE BURST` — the coverage member's third head-of-test self-check calls `Diff(desired, actual)` TWICE on the same inputs for every scenario and reflect.DeepEquals the resulting plans. A regression that introduced map-iteration ordering into the plan emission (e.g. a hashmap walk instead of the existing `sort.Strings(unionKeys)`) would surface here as a fast targeted failure on the affected scenario row, NOT as a confusing per-iteration burst mismatch many lines later. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits an ordered output from unordered inputs: pin determinism at the head of the test as its own self-check, separate from the per-row verdict; the property is structurally different from the closed-set tag and deserves its own seam. (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY IS GATED ON A row.seedValueMarker FLAG SO ONLY ROWS WHOSE MUTATOR ROUTES A VALUE THROUGH DesiredValue TRIGGER THE MARKER-SURVIVAL + MARKER-ABSENCE PREDICATE` — the value-free classification contract says env-var values reach Action ONLY via DesiredValue. The canary therefore seeds RECONCILESECRETMARKER ONLY in env-var-changed / env-var-missing scenarios (via the row's mutator, not a generic pre-mutator pass — important: a generic pass would bleed marker into the env-var-missing row's untouched baseline FOO value and produce an unwanted env_var_changed action, false-positiving the row's "missing" prediction). The predicate asserts (a) the marker survives in Action.DesiredValue of at least one update action, (b) the marker is absent from Action.Type / Kind / Reason / EnvVarKey, all ServiceRef ID fields, all DomainRef fields, AND all UnmanagedResource fields. Non-env-var rows (service_missing, domain_renamed, resource_unmanaged, etc.) leave the flag false and the canary is skipped — they exercise the classifier but route no value through DesiredValue. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set table covers multiple chokepoint output shapes and only SOME shapes route caller-supplied data through a specific output field: gate the canary per-row (a `row.seedValueMarker bool` flag), seed via the row's mutator (not a generic pass), and enumerate every output field that MUST NOT carry the value — the canary is a per-row contract, not a global one. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:412:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, store-layer pure-validator instance (BE-0404 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_break_glass_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/break_glass_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestBreakGlassCoversCallSites + TestBreakGlassPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Break-Glass Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: break-glass tests (BE-0404)" subsection; TWENTY-SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0403 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestBreakGlass ./...`, CI step name `Break-glass tests`, verify.sh prefix #28 inserted between BE-0403's #27 and the trailing optionals renumbered #28-#32 → #29-#33 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 32 → # 33` and synthetic-fixture `# 31 → # 32` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST store-layer pure-validator instance: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR LIVES IN PACKAGE store, NOT package store_test, SO IT REUSES nopAuditAppender{} + isInvalidInputErrorOn + breakGlassMaxTTL + breakGlassReasonMaxLen FROM internal_test.go DIRECTLY` — the existing white-box break-glass tests already declared `nopAuditAppender{}` in `internal_test.go` and `isInvalidInputErrorOn(err, field)` is a package-private predicate; the canonical pair stays package-internal so the validator chokepoint `buildSessionToCreate` is reachable without an exported-for-test seam. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose pair belongs in an existing white-box test package — keep package-internal so the contract test reuses the fixture surface for free. (2) `REDACTOR-SURVIVAL SELF-CHECK FIRES AT THE HEAD OF THE COVERS TEST, BEFORE THE TABLE WALK` — the marker `BREAKGLASSSECRETMARKER` is seeded into every scenario's Reason; accept rows assert the marker survives in `session.Reason` (the redactor must NOT strip it because it isn't a secret transport pattern), reject rows assert the marker never appears in `err.Error()`, `FieldViolation.Reason`, or `fmt.Sprintf("%+v", err)`. The head-of-test self-check probes `svc.redactor.Redact(marker)` and asserts the marker survives; without it, an over-aggressive redactor regression would silently false-positive every accept row's marker-survival assertion. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint feeds a redactor and asserts marker-survival on accept paths — probe the redactor's pure projection of the marker before walking the table; the probe is the chokepoint between "redactor leaves benign data intact" and "marker-survival predicate is meaningful". (3) `REJECT-PATH REDACTION CANARY SCANS THREE SURFACES: err.Error(), apierr.ViolationsOf(err) FieldViolation.Reason, AND fmt.Sprintf("%+v", err)` — yerr.Error's serialised form may stash data in metadata fields the bare Error() string does not expose; scanning all three closes the gap so a future regression that started stashing the offending value in error metadata (NOT just the message) fails the redaction predicate too. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose typed error carries field-violation metadata — scan every operator-visible projection of the error for the marker, not just the bare message. Two store-layer gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) `NewBreakGlassService` requires non-nil store/orgs/sessions/audit even when the validator path never reaches them — the constructor's nil-guard is structural; the canonical-service helper passes `&Store{}` + `NewOrganizationRepository()` + `NewBreakGlassRepository()` + `nopAuditAppender{}` as placeholders the validator never reaches. (B) `breakGlassDefaultTTL` MUST be strictly less than `breakGlassMaxTTL` so non-capping accept rows can assert `ExpiresAt - StartedAt == TTL` exactly; the ttl_capped accept row sets TTL = breakGlassMaxTTL+time.Hour explicitly. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:413:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, policy-engine pure-function instance (BE-0403 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_admin_endpoint_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/admin_endpoint_policy_test.go's new canonical pair TestAdminEndpointCoversCallSites + TestAdminEndpointPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Admin Endpoint Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: admin endpoint tests (BE-0403)" subsection; TWENTY-FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0402 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestAdminEndpoint ./...`, CI step name `Admin endpoint tests`, verify.sh prefix #27 inserted between BE-0402's #26 and the trailing optionals renumbered #27-#31 → #28-#32 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 31 → # 32` and synthetic-fixture `# 30 → # 31` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST policy-engine pure-function instance: (1) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE IS GENERATED FROM CARTESIAN PRODUCT, NOT HANDWRITTEN` — adminEndpointScenarios() builds 90 rows from 6 endpoints × (12 role rows + 3 grant rows) by nested for-loops over four structural axes (endpoint × role × {same-tenant, cross-tenant} × grant scope); each row carries its own predicted (Allow, Reason) so a failure points the operator at the exact axis that drifted. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose scenario table is the cartesian product of two or more closed enumerations — generate the table, never hand-list it; the per-row predicted verdict is the contract. (2) `THREE STRUCTURAL SELF-CHECKS FIRE FAST AT THE HEAD OF THE COVERS-CALL-SITES TEST, BEFORE THE TABLE WALK` — (a) every endpoint in adminEndpointCallSites binds to one of the four ActionAdmin* constants AND every ActionAdmin* is bound to ≥1 endpoint (exhaustiveness in both directions), (b) every admin action is catalogued as CapSupport via Engine.ActionCapability, (c) builtinRoleCaps admits CapSupport for exactly RoleSupport via BuiltinRoles(). Each fires before the row walk so a regression that drifted the catalog or role matrix gives a fast targeted failure at the seam, NOT a confusing per-row mismatch many lines later. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed set is constructed from registered enumerations the engine looks up by name — assert exhaustiveness in BOTH directions at the head of the test, then walk the table. (3) `REDACTION CANARY VIA SECRET MARKER IN PRINCIPAL ID, ASSERTED ON EVERY CONTENTION ITERATION` — adminEndpointPrincipal seeds the literal `ADMINENDPOINTSECRETMARKER` into every Principal ID, and the contention burst's per-iteration check calls reasonContainsSubstring(got.Reason, marker) to assert the Reason never echoes principal-supplied data. The Reason set is closed today so the check is structurally redundant, but it catches a future engine change that widened Reason to caller-supplied data on its FIRST iteration, NOT after a real credential leaks into a log. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint produces a structured outcome (Reason, Code, etc.) and is fed caller-supplied identifiers — seed a marker in the caller-supplied input and scan the output on every burst iteration; the marker is the chokepoint between "decides via" and "echoes the input". Two engine gotchas worth pinning forward: (A) Principal struct uses `Disabled bool` (default false = enabled), NOT an `Enabled` field — invented `Enabled:true` is a compile error; the zero-value-is-enabled invariant matches the existing principalIn helper. (B) Grant-only principals (Role="" + non-empty Grants) skip the unknown-role guard at engine.go:109 because the guard is conditional on `principal.Role != ""`; leave Role unset rather than setting a sentinel string the engine would reject. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:414:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-validator instance (BE-0402 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_config_validation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/config_validation_test.go's new canonical pair TestConfigValidationCoversCallSites + TestConfigValidationPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Config Validation Tests" section + a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: config validation tests (BE-0402)" subsection; TWENTY-FOURTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0401 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestConfigValidation ./...`, CI step name `Config validation tests`, verify.sh prefix #26 inserted between BE-0401's #25 and the trailing optionals renumbered #26-#30 → #27-#31 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 30 → # 31` and synthetic-fixture `# 29 → # 30` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-function-validator instance: (1) `SAME-PACKAGE TEST FILE REUSES EXISTING PACKAGE-INTERNAL FIXTURES` — the new file lives in `package config` (NOT `package config_test`) so it sees the existing `strictEnv()` helper, `MapLookup`, and every const + min/max length seam directly. No exported-for-test seam, no module-scope helper duplication. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose pair belongs in an existing test package — keep package-internal so the contract test reuses the fixture surface for free. (2) `SECRET REDACTION CANARY VIA SEEDED MARKER IN BASELINE ENV` — `configValidationMarkedEnv()` seeds the literal `SUPERSECRETMARKER` into every secret-bearing env (DSN / signing keys / Dokploy token), and `assessConfigValidationScenario` asserts no error string ever echoes the marker. This is the structural redaction predicate every operator log downstream of `config.Load` relies on; a future regression that started embedding the offending value in the error message would fail the predicate on its first scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint surfaces typed errors from secret-bearing inputs — seed a marker in the baseline fixture and assert its absence in every error message; the marker is the chokepoint between "names which field failed" and "echoes its value". (3) `BURST SIZE FOR PURE-FUNCTION VALIDATOR IS 1024..2048, MOD-INDEXED INTO THE CLOSED-SET TABLE` — `configValidationWorkers=32 * configValidationIterationsPerWorker=64 = 2048`, with each of the 30 scenarios drawn ~68 times via `idx := (w*iters + i) % len(scenarios)`. Smaller bursts would not reliably surface a future shared-mutable-state regression; larger bursts bloat wall-clock for a function that does no I/O. Forward-applicable to every pure-function verification gate — pick a burst in the 1024..2048 range and mod-index, never per-goroutine random selection (defeats the per-iteration prediction contract). Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:416:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, OPT-IN external-live-Dokploy-smoke instance (BE-0400 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_external_dokploy_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/live_dokploy_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLiveDokploySmokeRespectsOptInFlag + TestLiveDokploySmokeExercisesLiveEndpoint + SECURITY.md "## External Live-Dokploy Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table (with When = "Opt-in (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1`), nightly + manual", NOT "Every push and PR") + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: external live-Dokploy smoke tests behind an opt-in flag (BE-0400)" subsection; TWENTY-SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0399 verification-suite mega-pattern AND the FIRST opt-in instance. Canonical command `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1 go test -run TestLiveDokploySmoke ./...`, but the FIVE BE-0400-specific shifts from the on-every-PR template are forward-applicable to any future opt-in verification story: (1) `CANONICAL COMMAND LIVES IN verificationLoop.optionalWhenConfigured, NOT requiredBackendCommands — AND THE STATIC TEST ASSERTS BOTH PRESENCE IN OPTIONAL AND ABSENCE FROM REQUIRED` — a future drift that moved the smoke into requiredBackendCommands would break the default CI run on every push and PR (it would try to reach a real Dokploy that does not exist), so the static test pins the negative assertion as a load-bearing contract; the matcher's `inRequired` self-check fixture deliberately places the command in requiredBackendCommands to exercise the refusal path. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: place the command in optionalWhenConfigured AND assert it stays out of requiredBackendCommands. (2) `CI SURFACE IS A DEDICATED WORKFLOW FILE (.github/workflows/external-smoke.yml) WITH workflow_dispatch + nightly schedule TRIGGERS AND AN EXPLICIT ENV-INJECTION LITERAL, NOT A STEP IN ci.yml's TEST JOB` — the static test pins five workflow substrings simultaneously: the canonical step name `- name: External live-Dokploy smoke tests`, the canonical run command `go test -run TestLiveDokploySmoke ./...`, the `workflow_dispatch:` trigger, the `schedule:` trigger, and the `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY: "1"` env injection. Pinning the env-injection literal is essential: without it, the smoke would t.Skip silently and the workflow would always report green without ever reaching Dokploy. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: pin BOTH triggers AND the env-injection literal in the same matcher; the trigger + opt-in pair is what makes the workflow's nightly run actually exercise the gate. (3) `VERIFY.SH OPTIONAL STEP GATED ON THE SAME ENV VAR THE TEST READS AT RUNTIME` — the verify.sh step's `if [[ "${YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY:-}" = "1" ]]; then` guard ensures the optional step is skipped on developer laptops without the opt-in, but exercises the identical command CI runs when the operator does opt in. The static test pins BOTH the canonical step header `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` AND the literal command `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1 go test -run TestLiveDokploySmoke ./...` — the leading env prefix is part of the contract because verify.sh's step header is a comment, not an env-injection line, so the env prefix MUST appear in the command itself. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: gate the verify.sh optional step on the SAME env var the test reads at runtime and pin the env-prefixed command literal as part of the contract. (4) `CONTRIBUTING.md ENTRY LIVES IN A NEW "Opt-in external suites" SUBSECTION UNDER THE EXISTING OPTIONAL TOOLS BLOCK, NOT THE NUMBERED REQUIRED LIST` — the numbered required list (1-24 after BE-0399) is reserved for the on-every-PR gates; opt-in suites get a separate subsection so contributors see the cadence boundary at a glance. The static test pins only the canonical entry literal (the command with surrounding backticks) so the analyser is robust to Markdown line wrap and prose changes around it. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: add the entry to the Optional Tools subsection (creating it if needed), and pin only the canonical command literal in the static test, not the surrounding prose. (5) `SECURITY.md ROW's When COLUMN SAYS "Opt-in (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1`), nightly + manual", NOT THE UNIFORM "Every push and PR" USED BY EVERY OTHER GATE` — the When column is the operator-facing signal that distinguishes the opt-in cadence from the on-every-PR cadence. The static test pins the entire row literal so a regression that flipped the cadence to "Every push and PR" would fail the matcher; a regression that dropped the row entirely would also fail. The dedicated `## External Live-Dokploy Smoke Tests` section's 16 substring set includes a NEW invariant set distinct from BE-0399: the documented env vars (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_BASE_URL, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_TOKEN), the cadence framing (opt-in, nightly), the canonical workflow path (.github/workflows/external-smoke.yml), the typed error code surface (yalla.error.v1), the redaction contract (redacted), the canonical function pair, and the self-locating static-test filename. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: pin the When-column literal AND a dedicated section with env-var + cadence + workflow-path substrings. Load-bearing invariant unchanged from BE-0379..BE-0399: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. ALSO: the smoke test's canonical pair uses a NON-CONTENTION shape (closed-set opt-in flag values + live-path-with-deterministic-redaction-branch) instead of the closed-set-coverage + per-decision-stability-burst shape of BE-0379..BE-0399 — appropriate for a SMOKE whose "live" branch is by definition external, where firing thousands of goroutines at a real Dokploy would be hostile to the upstream and meaningless under the race detector. Forward-applicable to every smoke / opt-in story whose live path is external infrastructure: drop the contention burst, replace it with a deterministic redaction-contract branch (httptest.NewServer that echoes a sentinel token; assert the typed wire error AND the absence of the token in the error chain) so the redaction property is enforced on every push and PR even when the live path is skipped. ALSO: the Dokploy client maps a 401/403 from the upstream to `apierr.Internal()` → `yerr.CodeInternal` (Yalla's OWN credentials rejected = internal Yalla misconfiguration, NOT customer-actionable auth failure) — pinning this in the synthetic-server branch is itself a load-bearing contract worth pinning across any future Dokploy-touching smoke.
ralph/progress.txt:417:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, backup-restore-rehearsal instance (BE-0399 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_backup_restore_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/backup_restore_rehearsal_test.go's new canonical pair TestBackupRestoreRehearsalCoversCallSites + TestBackupRestoreRehearsalPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Backup Restore Rehearsal Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: backup and restore rehearsal tests (BE-0399)" subsection; TWENTY-FIRST worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0398 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestBackupRestoreRehearsal ./...`, CI step name `Backup restore rehearsal tests`, verify.sh prefix #24 inserted between BE-0398's #23 and the trailing optionals renumbered 24->25/25->26/26->27/27->28 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0398's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 18 substrings: internal/controlplane/backup, backup_restore_rehearsal_test.go, deterministic, FileReporter, NewFileReporter, ErrNoBackupRecorded, Status.Fresh, MaxAge, RFC3339, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, rehearsal, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0399-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a single-file leaf-package state-reporting chokepoint with a documented closed set of error sentinels and a tiny external-input parser (BE-0400 external Dokploy smoke, BE-0401 release build, BE-0402 config validation): (1) `SCENARIO-TABLE-DRIVEN MULTI-SHAPE OBSERVATION VERIFIER — ONE assess() FUNCTION FEEDS BOTH MEMBERS, RETURNS REASON STRINGS NOT t.Errorf CALLS` — the canonical pair shares an `assessRehearsalObservation(scen, status, err) []string` helper that takes a scenario and an (output, error) tuple and returns a list of human-readable failure reasons. The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests and calls t.Errorf per reason inline; the burst member's worker goroutines call the same helper but record (worker, iteration, scenario, reasons) into a mutex-guarded slice that the main goroutine drains and reports after wg.Wait() — no testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged), same shape as BE-0395/BE-0396/BE-0397's burst patterns. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set table feeds both members AND the chokepoint surfaces a tuple (status, err) instead of a single verdict: a single string-returning assess() function is the source-of-truth predicate; iterate it in subtests for coverage, batch-collect-and-report for burst. (2) `CLOSED-SET ERROR-SHAPE ENUMERATION VIA TYPED KIND CONSTANT (Nil / Sentinel / Typed-yerr.Code / context.Canceled) RATHER THAN PER-SCENARIO ERROR LITERAL` — the rehearsalErrKind iota enumerates the four error shapes the reporter is documented to surface (rehearsalErrNil, rehearsalErrSentinelNoBackup, rehearsalErrTypedCodeServer, rehearsalErrContextCanceled). The scenario's wantErr field carries the kind, and assess() switches on the kind to choose the right errors.Is/errors.As predicate. Asserting on the kind (not on a literal error message) keeps the test stable across cosmetic message edits while still pinning the load-bearing typed-error contract. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint returns a documented closed-set error: don't hardcode literal messages — enumerate the kinds and switch on them in the verifier. (3) `SHARED REPORTER PER SCENARIO ACROSS BURST GOROUTINES — NOT FRESH BUILD PER GOROUTINE — TO PROVE THE DOCUMENTED CONCURRENT-SAFE CONTRACT` — backup.FileReporter is documented safe for concurrent use; building a fresh reporter per goroutine would not exercise that contract. The burst member builds one fixture per scenario UP FRONT (each fixture pairs the scenario with a built reporter), then every goroutine that draws scenario i shares the SAME reporter. A regression that introduced a shared mutable state (a memo cache, a non-snapshot read) in the reporter would surface as a goroutine seeing the WRONG scenario's predicate. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented safe for concurrent use: share the chokepoint instance across burst goroutines drawing the same scenario, never build fresh per goroutine — the burst's value is precisely the contention against the SAME instance.
ralph/progress.txt:418:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, tenant-isolation instance (BE-0398 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_tenant_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/tenant_isolation_test.go's new canonical pair TestTenantIsolationCoversCallSites + TestTenantIsolationPreservesScopeUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Tenant Isolation Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: tenant isolation tests (BE-0398)" subsection; TWENTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0397 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestTenantIsolation ./...`, CI step name `Tenant isolation tests`, verify.sh prefix #23 inserted between BE-0397's #22 and the trailing optionals renumbered 23->24/24->25/25->26/26->27 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0397's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 20 substrings: internal/controlplane/policy, tenant_isolation_test.go, deterministic, policy.Engine, NewEngine, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, ReasonAllowedBySupport, ReasonAllowedSelf, ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal, ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, ReasonDeniedUnknownAction, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0398-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is an authorization / policy / RBAC chokepoint in a Go package with an existing matrix-style test fixture (BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal, BE-0400 external-dokploy-smoke): (1) `SINGLE-PACKAGE INTERNAL TEST REUSING THE EXISTING TENANT-CONSTANT FIXTURES — NO NEW STORE / FAKE / RECORDER NEEDED` — the canonical pair lives in `package policy` (the SAME internal test package as the existing `policy_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `orgA` / `orgB` constants, the `principalIn` / `resourceIn` / `assertDecision` helpers, and the package-private `builtinRoleCaps` matrix verbatim. Unlike BE-0395..BE-0397 (which each introduced a new in-file concurrent fixture for the burst member because the chokepoint had observable mutable state), policy.Engine.Decide is a documented pure function with no I/O and no shared mutable state; the contention burst therefore needs no fixture at all — every goroutine constructs its own (Principal, Action, Resource) tuple from the closed-set coverage table and asserts the verdict matches the table's prediction for ITS OWN tuple. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a pure function: skip the concurrent-fixture pattern entirely; let the goroutines share the immutable Engine and assert per-iteration verdict-vs-prediction equality. (2) `THE COVERAGE TABLE AND THE BURST PREDICTION SHARE ONE SOURCE-OF-TRUTH FUNCTION` — buildTenantIsolationScenarios(t) returns a []tenantIsolationScenario where each entry pairs the input tuple with the predicted (wantAllow, wantReason). The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests; the burst member draws from the SAME table (indexed by `(w * iters + i) % len(scenarios)`) and asserts the engine's runtime verdict matches `scen.wantAllow / scen.wantReason` for the SPECIFIC tuple THIS goroutine drew. A cross-write under the race that swapped two goroutines' tuples would yield a verdict that matches the OTHER goroutine's prediction (not THIS one's), and the per-iteration assertion would fail with the offending scenario name. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set scenario table feeds both a coverage table AND a contention burst: derive both from the same builder, key the burst's per-iteration assertion on the BURST's own tuple's expectation (not the aggregate count), and surface the worker + iteration index + scenario name on every per-iteration failure so an operator can correlate the regression to a specific cross-write. (3) `ASYMMETRIC AUTHORIZE-ERROR REDACTION CONTRACT — REASON CODE IN MESSAGE ONLY FOR FORBIDDEN, NOT FOR UNAUTHENTICATED` — policy.Engine.Authorize maps ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal to apierr.Unauthenticated (HTTP 401) with a fixed `"authentication is required for action <action>"` message that does NOT echo the reason code; every other deny reason maps to apierr.Forbidden (HTTP 403) with `"not authorized for action <action> (<reason>)"`. The tenant-isolation coverage member's redaction sub-assertion therefore checks the action substring on every denial but only checks the reason substring on the Forbidden branch (skipping ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal). A regression that started echoing principal/tenant ids into either message would still surface — the per-secret loop covers both branches. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose authorization chokepoint surfaces multiple error envelopes: enumerate which fields are guaranteed in which envelope and pin the assertions per-branch, not as a single message-shape check.
ralph/progress.txt:419:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pagination-stability instance (BE-0397 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_pagination_stability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/pagination/pagination_stability_test.go's new canonical pair TestPaginationStabilityCoversCallSites + TestPaginationStabilityPreservesPagesUnderInserts + SECURITY.md "## Pagination Stability Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: pagination stability tests (BE-0397)" subsection; NINETEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0396 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestPaginationStability ./...`, CI step name `Pagination stability tests`, verify.sh prefix #22 inserted between BE-0396's #21 and the trailing optionals renumbered 22->23/23->24/24->25/25->26 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0396's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 19 substrings: internal/controlplane/pagination, pagination_stability_test.go, deterministic, fakeStore, concurrentPaginationStore, EncodeCursor, DecodeCursor, base64url, no duplicates, no drops, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0397-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a list-pagination / cursor-encoding / wire-shape invariant in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture (BE-0398 tenant isolation, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal): (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING ZERO-MUTEX SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL MUTEX-GUARDED FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package pagination` (the same internal package as the existing `page_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeStore` / `row` / `positionByID` / `rowID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file store, `concurrentPaginationStore`, with a `sync.RWMutex`-guarded rows slice and `insert` / `delete` / `list` / `listAsc` methods — declaring it in the new test file (not in `page_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeStore`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. (2) `WIDEN THE EXISTING FIXTURE WITH AN ASCENDING SIBLING METHOD WHEN THE COVERAGE SCENARIO TABLE NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS — KEEP THE DESCENDING-DEFAULT METHOD UNCHANGED` — the existing `fakeStore.list` hard-codes descending order (matches the audit-log / deployment-list canonical case). Rather than refactor it (and risk regressing the existing TestCursor* tests), add a sibling `listAsc` method on the same receiver in the new test file so the closed-set coverage member can iterate both directions through a `storeLike` interface (`list` + `listAsc`) without forking the descending fixture. The interface lives in the new test file so the existing `fakeStore` definition file is untouched. (3) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE COVERS EVERY DISTINCT PAGINATION SHAPE — EMPTY / SINGLE-ROW / EXACT-PAGE / MULTI-PAGE-NO-REMAINDER / MULTI-PAGE-PARTIAL-TRAILING / SMALL-PAGE / BOTH DIRECTIONS` — the scenario-table-rejects-duplicate-names self-check at the head of the coverage member guards against a future addition that quietly drops the closed-set property. Each scenario walks BuildPage end-to-end with a safety-bound terminator (1000-page bound) so the test fails loudly if a regression introduces a non-terminating cursor stream; the diagnostic surfaces both the scenario name AND the page index AND the row id of the divergence so an operator can correlate the gate failure with a specific in-flight traversal without re-running the suite locally. The contention burst sizing (workers=4, iterations=8, seedRows=50, pageSize=5) is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe store fails under -race ~99% of the time while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI.
ralph/progress.txt:420:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, audit-completeness instance (BE-0396 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_audit_completeness_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/audit/audit_completeness_test.go's new canonical pair TestAuditCompletenessCoversCallSites + TestAuditCompletenessPreservesRecordedFieldsUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Audit Completeness Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: audit completeness tests (BE-0396)" subsection; EIGHTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0395 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestAuditCompleteness ./...`, CI step name `Audit completeness tests`, verify.sh prefix #21 inserted between BE-0395's #20 and the trailing optionals renumbered 21->22/22->23/23->24/24->25 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0395's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0396 substring count = 18; matches BE-0395 count — the scope swaps three idempotency-specific anchors `Idempotency-Replayed` / `Idempotency-Key` / `fakeIdempotencyStore` for three audit-specific anchors `Auditor.Record` / `policy.Action` / `policy.Decision`, and swaps `byte-identical` / `E_INVALID_INPUT` / `E_FORBIDDEN` / `E_NOT_FOUND` for `allowed` / `denied` / `concurrentAuditRecorder` / `fakeRecorder`), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0396-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a chokepoint-mediated event-recording surface (audit / metrics / structured-log / outbox emitter) in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture: (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL CONCURRENT FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package audit_test` (the same external test package as the existing `audit_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeRecorder` / `newAuditor` / `ctxWith` / `orgID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file recorder, `concurrentAuditRecorder`, with a mutex-guarded events slice and a `snapshot()` accessor — declaring it in the new test file (not in `audit_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeRecorder`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst would race the existing single-call fixture: don't retrofit a mutex onto the existing helper — add a sibling helper in the new test file. The BE-0395 pattern called for "extend, don't fork" the existing fixtures; BE-0396 refines that to "extend the coverage helper, add a sibling for the contention burst" when the existing helper's zero-mutex shape is part of its contract. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE WALKS ALLOWED *AND* DENIED VARIANTS PER ACTION SURFACE` — the audit-completeness contract is not "every recorded event is well-formed" — it's "every security-relevant decision IS recorded AND well-formed", and denied decisions are as load-bearing as allowed ones (an audit log that omits denials is incomplete by definition). The scenario table therefore enumerates the canonical policy.Action surface (organization.create allowed, project.create allowed, environment.create allowed, service.create allowed, project.delete denied cross-tenant, environment.delete denied out-of-scope, keys.manage denied no-capability, limits.write denied principal-disabled) and the per-scenario `wantDecision` field pins the recorded `store.AuditDecision` to `AuditDecisionAllowed` or `AuditDecisionDenied` so a regression that flipped the recorder's verdict literal trips per-scenario. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is a chokepoint that records BOTH outcomes (audit / metrics counter / structured log emitter / outbox): the closed-set scenario table MUST cover both verdicts, not just the happy path. (3) `PER-EMITTER FIELD-FIDELITY UNDER CONTENTION VIA EXPECTED-SET / OBSERVED-SET DIFF` — the runtime burst seeds `auditCompletenessWorkers * auditCompletenessIterationsPerWorker = 32` concurrent goroutines, each emitting a recorded decision with a unique `req_audit_completeness_burst_<w>_<i>` request_id. After the burst, the test builds two maps (expected: the closed set of synthesized request_ids; observed: the set of request_ids on captured events) and reports both missing-in-observed (a dropped emission OR a cross-write that overwrote one emitter's RequestID with another's — both surface here) AND unexpected-in-observed (a spurious event OR a cross-write that produced a new request_id under contention). Asserting on the SET (not just the count) is what catches the cross-write regression — a recorder that swapped two emitters' RequestIDs under a race would pass the count assertion but fail the set diff. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose contention burst's contract is "every emitter's identifying field is preserved on its recorded event": build expected/observed sets keyed on the emitter-unique identifier, then diff both ways. The burst's auditWorkers=4 × auditIters=8 sizing matches the BE-0395 burst sizing (32 concurrent calls is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe recorder fails under -race ~99% of the time, while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI).
ralph/progress.txt:421:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, idempotency-replay instance (BE-0395 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_idempotency_replay_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/idempotency_replay_test.go's new canonical pair TestIdempotencyReplayCoversCallSites + TestIdempotencyReplayPreservesByteIdenticalEnvelope + SECURITY.md "## Idempotency Replay Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: idempotency replay tests (BE-0395)" subsection; SEVENTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0394 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestIdempotencyReplay ./...`, CI step name `Idempotency replay tests`, verify.sh prefix #20 inserted between BE-0394's #19 and the trailing optionals renumbered 20->21/21->22/22->23/23->24 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0394's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0395 substring count = 18; up from BE-0394's 16 because the idempotency-replay scope adds three closed-set error codes E_INVALID_INPUT / E_FORBIDDEN / E_NOT_FOUND and the Idempotency-Replayed/Idempotency-Key wire-header pair, and drops the chaos-Postgres-specific fakepg / DisconnectFault / yerr.CodeUnavailable / apierr.DependencyStore / apierr.StoreUnavailable constants in favour of the replay-specific fakeIdempotencyStore and byte-identical anchors), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0395-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same Go package: (1) `EXISTING IN-PACKAGE TEST FIXTURES MUST BE REUSED, NOT FORKED` — the canonical pair lives in `package httpapi` (not `httpapi_test`) deliberately so the existing newFakeIdempotencyStore, idemRef, recordingHandler, jsonHandler, postReq, run, decodeError, and idemPrincipal helpers from idempotency_test.go and middleware_test.go are reused verbatim. The closed-set bound on Claim/Complete/Release counters is part of the fakeIdempotencyStore contract and a forked helper would silently break that bound. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same package: extend, don't fork. The siblings BE-0396..BE-0404 should look for an existing `fake<Subject>Store` / `recording<Subject>` helper in the same package before adding a new one — the prior security-verification BE-0344+BE-0345 pattern said the same about handler-builders. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE MUST DOCUMENT THE EXCLUSION` — the replay closed set is success + 400/403/404 error envelopes; 5xx is deliberately excluded because the middleware releases the claim on 5xx (the contract is TestRequireIdempotencyServerErrorIsNotRecorded). Both the canonical pair's package-doc block AND the SECURITY.md section's Scope paragraph spell out the exclusion explicitly, so a future reader understands the asymmetry without re-reading the middleware. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set scenario table has a deliberate gap: name the gap in the section comment + SECURITY.md so a future contributor doesn't "fix" the apparent omission. (3) `BYTE-LITERAL RECORDED BODY, NOT STRUCTURAL JSON COMPARISON` — the runtime burst seeds a literal byte slice `{"schema_version":"yalla.output.v1","ok":true,...}` and asserts `got.body == string(recorded)`, not reflect.DeepEqual on json.Unmarshal'd maps. A structural comparison would mask a whitespace drift (an envelope re-rendered on replay would parse semantically equivalent but byte-different). Forward-applicable to every replay/recording invariant: assert on bytes, not parsed structures, when the contract is byte-identical determinism. Burst sizing: replayWorkers=4 × replayIterationsPerWorker=8 = 32 concurrent retries per run, enough to surface a non-deterministic recorder under -race without bloating CI wall-clock. The runtime burst observes per-iteration {status, body, header} and asserts post-burst aggregation in the main goroutine (no testing.T calls from workers — same shape as BE-0392's burst pattern).
ralph/progress.txt:422:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Postgres-disconnects instance (BE-0394 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_postgres_disconnects_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/chaos_postgres_disconnects_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsCoversCallSites + TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsMapsToTypedUnavailableEnvelope + new internal/controlplane/store/fakepg package (Server + DisconnectFault + NewServer) + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Postgres Disconnects" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Postgres disconnects (BE-0394)" subsection; SIXTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0393 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosPostgresDisconnects ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Postgres disconnects`, verify.sh prefix #19 inserted between BE-0393's #18 and the trailing optionals renumbered 19->20/20->21/21->22/22->23 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0393's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0394 substring count = 16; matches BE-0393's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0394-specific extensions to the chaos-classification template, all forward-applicable to ANY future chaos-Postgres / chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit sibling whose subject is the store layer or any pgxpool-backed dependency: (1) `NEW IN-PROCESS FAKE NETWORK ENDPOINT FOR PGX` — unlike BE-0393's dokployfake (which exists as a long-standing HTTP test double under internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake), there was no fakepg before this story. The minimal viable shape is a TCP listener on 127.0.0.1:0 that does NOT speak the Postgres wire protocol — it accepts the TCP connection (so pgx's dial completes successfully — important: graceful FIN, NOT RST via SetLinger(0), because RST races the dial on some kernels and surfaces as `connection reset by peer` on connect rather than the production failure mode of "dial succeeds, handshake fails") and then closes the conn, so pgx observes EOF on its startup-handshake read. The fake exposes a FIFO Fault queue (only DisconnectFault for now), a Host()/Port()/Addr() trio for DSN construction, an AcceptedConnections() counter so the harness can assert pgx made >=1 TCP attempt per op (a regression that short-circuited pgx without ever attempting a network connect would otherwise pass silently), Close() (idempotent), New(testing.TB) for tests inside the testing package, and NewServer() error for worker-goroutine constructors that cannot satisfy testing.TB. Forward-applicable to every future chaos-Postgres sibling: add SQLErrorFault (writes an ErrorResponse before closing — would require pgproto3 dependency from pgx), LatencyFault (delays the close), AuthFailFault (writes a FATAL ErrorResponse during startup). (2) `CLASSIFIER-WRAP CONTRACT, NOT END-TO-END CALLSITE WRAP` — unlike BE-0393's chaos test which exercises real production methods on the typed Dokploy client (c.GetDeployment, c.EnsureOrganization, c.RemoveService) so the test asserts on errors the client's PRODUCTION wrapping chokepoint already produced, BE-0394's chaos test exercises raw pgxpool ops (Pool.Ping, Pool.Acquire, Pool.Begin, Pool.Exec, Pool.Query) and EXPLICITLY wraps the resulting pgx error through apierr.StoreUnavailable inside the runtime member before asserting the contract. This is because the store layer does not currently route every call site through a single classifier chokepoint the way the Dokploy client does — store call sites return raw pgx errors and the wrapping happens at handler-shim boundaries. The chaos contract therefore pins the wrapper's behaviour given realistic pgx-derived errors, not every call site's wrapping behaviour. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose subject lacks a single production classifier chokepoint: assert the wrapper given realistic causes, not the production path. When a future story (BE-04xx) adds a store-layer classifier chokepoint, the chaos test can switch to invoking that chokepoint instead — the scenario table and the redaction/dependency/code assertions stay identical. (3) `ASYMMETRIC REDACTION CONTRACT — PASSWORD AT EVERY LEVEL, USERNAME AT WRAPPER ONLY` — pgx's connect error formatter intentionally includes the DSN username in error messages (for operator debuggability) but redacts the password. The chaos test's chaosPostgresSecretMarkers (cause-chain scan) therefore lists ONLY the password sentinel; the wrapper-envelope assertion (chaosPostgresEnvelopeMarkers) lists BOTH the password and the username sentinel because apierr.StoreUnavailable's rendered message is static ("the Yalla datastore is temporarily unavailable") and MUST NOT echo ANY pgx cause-chain content. The runtime member walks errors.Unwrap chain for the password marker AND asserts the wrapper envelope's rendered Error() string for both markers. Forward-applicable: any future chaos test whose subject is a wire-protocol library that includes debug info in errors should split the redaction contract into chain-level (real secrets only) + envelope-level (anything pgx-style libraries might leak into debug strings). The sentinel-literal approach (chaosPostgresSentinelPassword="yallachaossecret_DO_NOT_LEAK_42") keeps the assertion meaningful: if the wrapper EVER started embedding the cause's message, the sentinel would surface and the gate would trip on the first observation. Per-iteration setup cost is bounded by chaosPostgresConnectTimeout=750ms (per-attempt pgx connect deadline) which is strictly less than chaosPostgresOpTimeout=2s (per-op context deadline), so the burst (5 scenarios × 4 workers × 3 iterations = 60 iterations) completes in ~0.5s wall-clock.
ralph/progress.txt:423:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Dokploy-timeouts instance (BE-0393 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_dokploy_timeouts_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/chaos_dokploy_timeouts_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosDokployTimeoutsCoversCallSites + TestChaosDokployTimeoutsMapsToTypedTimeoutEnvelope + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Dokploy Timeouts" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Dokploy timeouts (BE-0393)" subsection; FIFTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0392 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosDokployTimeouts ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Dokploy timeouts`, verify.sh prefix #18 inserted between BE-0392's #17 and the trailing optionals renumbered 18->19/19->20/20->21/21->22 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0392's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0393 substring count = 16; one fewer than BE-0392 because the chaos suite is package-bounded to internal/controlplane/dokploy and does not need a separate /healthz//readyz//version path enumeration — instead it names `TimeoutFault`, `yerr.CodeTimeout`, and `apierr.DependencyDokploy` as the load-bearing classification anchors), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Additional pattern member specific to chaos-classification verification stories: the canonical pair member that runs the runtime burst constructs a FRESH fake-Dokploy server per iteration (not per goroutine) via runChaosIteration so the FIFO fault queue and the recorded-request buffer stay isolated; this avoids the shared-queue contention that would otherwise turn a per-scenario expectedAttempts assertion into a flake under concurrent goroutines. Forward-applicable to every BE-039x chaos-timeout sibling whose invariant is `client classifies X dependency as typed *yerr.Error with Code=Y under fault Z` (BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, future chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit suites). The fixture-table closed-set member's per-scenario knobs — name, method, expectedAttempts, expectedCode, expectedDependency, call closure — are the reusable shape; the BE-0394 Postgres-disconnect sibling extends this by swapping the fake-Dokploy `TimeoutFault` for a fakepg.DisconnectFault and the typed-client surface for the store layer's pgx pool, keeping every other knob.
ralph/progress.txt:424:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, load-smoke instance (BE-0392 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_load_smoke_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/load_smoke_test.go's new canonical pair TestLoadSmokeContractCoversCoreEndpoints + TestLoadSmokeContractRunsBurstWithStableEnvelopes + SECURITY.md "## Load Smoke Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: load smoke tests (BE-0392)" subsection; FOURTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0391 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestLoadSmoke ./...`, CI step name `Load smoke tests`, verify.sh prefix #17 inserted between BE-0390's #16 and the optionals — optionals renumbered #17→#18 govulncheck, #18→#19 staticcheck, #19→#20 golangci-lint, #20→#21 goreleaser in the SAME edit; TENTH consecutive required-step insertion with optional renumbering since BE-0380's #6). Two BE-0392-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing subject is an HTTP handler under burst load: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × DETERMINISTIC DUAL — NO POSTGRES MEMBER` — unlike BE-0389/BE-0390 whose canonical pair splits across deterministic-fixture-only + Postgres-required substrates, BE-0392's BOTH pair members are deterministic. The bootstrap surface (`/healthz`, `/readyz`, `/version`) is the one part of the public HTTP API that needs no backing infrastructure — no Postgres, no fake Dokploy fixtures, no quota counters mutated, no scope-resolution chain. The closed-set member walks an in-memory endpoint table; the burst member spins the test handler in-process via httptest.NewServer and fires 16 workers × 32 iterations = 512 concurrent requests per endpoint. Neither member calls t.Skip on missing env vars — the gate trips on every developer machine and every CI runner without setup. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a stateless HTTP surface or a pure-in-process invariant (BE-0407 desired-state goldens against in-memory reconciler, BE-0398 tenant isolation matrix at the policy layer alone). Critical implication: the BE-0389/BE-0390 dual-substrate shape is NOT mandatory for the canonical-pair template — when both invariants are deterministic, the pair can stay deterministic-only and the SECURITY.md section's "skips when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset" language is OMITTED (BE-0392's section instead explicitly says "no Postgres, no live Dokploy server"). The shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` opt-in-external substring stays in the substring set as the cross-suite escape hatch reference, but does NOT bind to any code path the canonical pair member exercises. (2) `BURST OBSERVATION COLLECTION SHAPE — channel buffered to total request count, observation struct includes endpoint + status + schema + ok + requestID + bodyBad + headerBad, then post-burst aggregation in main goroutine` — the burst test must avoid testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged); instead each goroutine pushes a typed `observation{}` onto a buffered channel sized to `len(endpoints) * workers * iterations` and the main goroutine drains the channel after wg.Wait()+close. Aggregation builds (a) a per-endpoint count map asserted against the expected burst total, (b) a per-request-id map for uniqueness assertion, (c) per-observation envelope/status/redaction assertions. Failure messages quote the offending endpoint AND the observed request_id (or "<missing>" sentinel + a synthetic "<request-construction-failed:...>" / "<request-do-failed:...>" id when the round-trip itself failed) so the operator correlates against logs without re-running. The burst total (16 × 32 × 3 = 1536 requests) completes in ~600ms on a developer laptop — safely under the flaky-test threshold. Forward-applicable to BE-0393 (chaos Dokploy timeouts — same channel+aggregation shape, but with a fault-injection step before each iteration), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — same shape but with t.Skip on YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — same shape but each iteration writes the same Idempotency-Key and asserts every response after the first is the cached envelope). The closed-set bootstrap endpoint table includes a `wantStatus` field per entry so a future endpoint that legitimately returns 503 under some condition can be added without breaking the burst member's status assertion. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0390's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0392 substring count = 17; matches BE-0389's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:425:- Verification-suite UMBRELLA-shape adaptation, race-detector instance (BE-0391 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_race_detector_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Race Detector" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: race detector tests (BE-0391)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the umbrella shape — first was BE-0379's `go test ./...` — and THIRTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0390 verification-suite mega-pattern overall, canonical command `go test -race ./...`, CI step name `Race detector`, verify.sh prefix #5 STABLE since BE-0379). Three BE-0391-specific extensions to the template, all forward-applicable to ANY future umbrella verification story whose canonical command is a flag (`-race`, `-msan`, `-asan`, `-cover`) or an unfiltered `./...` rather than a `-run` filter: (1) `NO CANONICAL TEST-FUNCTION PAIR` — the `-race` flag wraps every `Test*` function the suite runs at the Go runtime layer, so there is no function-name prefix to pin (unlike BE-0383..BE-0390 whose `-run TestXxx` filter binds to a specific prefix). The umbrella shape mirrors BE-0379's `go test ./...` gate, but BE-0379 had `allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests` + `findPackagesMissingTests` as its analog "closed-set" surface (every package MUST have a *_test.go file OR be in the exemption set); BE-0391 has NEITHER closed-set surface because every package's tests are AUTOMATICALLY in scope when `-race` is set — there is nothing for the test to enumerate. The static test pins exactly 5 surfaces (no canonical-pair surface = 5 not 6): ci.yml step, verify.sh step, CONTRIBUTING entry, SECURITY row + section, PRD command(s). Forward-applicable to any future flag-style umbrella gate (race detector variants, msan, asan, coverage thresholds). (2) `TWO-ENTRY PRD PIN — `requiredLocalCommands` AND `requiredBackendCommands`` — unlike every prior verification-suite story (BE-0379..BE-0390) that pinned a single PRD command entry (BE-0379 in requiredLocalCommands, BE-0380..BE-0390 in requiredBackendCommands), BE-0391 pins BOTH: `go test -race ./...` in requiredLocalCommands AND `go test -race ./internal/controlplane/...` in requiredBackendCommands. The umbrella command is the canonical gate; the backend-narrowed command is the faster mid-iteration variant an agent can run without paying the full umbrella cost. Both are part of the contract because each serves a different agent workflow (final pre-commit gate vs. mid-iteration check). The two-entry pin is forward-applicable to ANY future verification gate whose canonical command has a naturally faster narrow variant (e.g. a future `go test -msan ./...` gate would also pin a `go test -msan ./internal/controlplane/...` faster variant). The matcher uses a shared `containsString([]string, string) bool` helper (declared in verification_suite_race_detector_static_test.go) instead of inlining the loop — this helper is now a reusable release_test package primitive future PRD-array matchers can call. (3) `STABLE PRE-EXISTING NUMERIC SLOT — NO RENUMBER NEEDED` — unlike BE-0380..BE-0390 each of which inserted a NEW required step into verify.sh and renumbered the trailing optionals in the same edit (NINE consecutive insertions from BE-0380's #6 to BE-0390's #16), BE-0391's slot #5 was STABLE since BE-0379's foundation work — verify.sh, CONTRIBUTING.md, and ci.yml already carried the race-detector step / header / entry. The BE-0391 work was purely DEFENSIVE: it added the static test that PINS those pre-existing surfaces so a future regression (rename, renumber, deletion) trips the gate. Critical implication: when a verification story's surfaces ALREADY EXIST at story start (because they were authored as foundation work before the verification-suite mega-pattern landed), the implementation is JUST the new static test + new SECURITY.md section + new AGENTS.md subsection + PRD flag flip — no ci.yml / verify.sh / CONTRIBUTING.md edits at all. Forward-applicable to any future verification story whose canonical command predates the mega-pattern (BE-0379's `go test ./...`, BE-0391's `go test -race ./...`, future `go vet ./...` / `gofmt -l .` umbrellas if those ever get verification-suite stories). The SECURITY.md substring set adapts: race-detector substring set has 11 entries (vs BE-0389's 17) because the umbrella gate has fewer per-suite-specific literals to pin — no canonical function pair to quote, no `-run` filter pivot, no per-suite env var beyond the shared `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`. The 11 substrings anchor on the umbrella contract pivots: `data race`, `go test -race`, `Every push and PR`, `deterministic`, `request_id`, `fake Dokploy`, `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`, `redacted`, `yalla.output.v1`, `yalla.error.v1`, and the self-locating file path. NOT applicable to focused `-run`-filter verification stories (BE-0392 load smoke, BE-0393 chaos Dokploy timeouts, BE-0394 chaos Postgres disconnects, BE-0395 idempotency replay, etc.) — those follow the BE-0389/BE-0390 canonical-pair shape; the umbrella shape applies ONLY when the canonical command itself is a runtime-flag or unfiltered umbrella.
ralph/progress.txt:426:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, migrations-from-empty-DB instance (BE-0389 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_migrations_empty_db_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrate_test.go's new canonical pair TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles + TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase + SECURITY.md "## Migration Tests From Empty DB" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: migration tests from empty DB (BE-0389)" subsection; ELEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0388 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...`, CI step name `Migration tests from empty DB`, verify.sh prefix #15). Four BE-0389-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR ENCODES DETERMINISTIC × POSTGRES-REQUIRED DUAL` — unlike every prior verification-suite story whose canonical pair pinned two invariants on the same execution substrate, BE-0389's pair splits across two SUBSTRATES: TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles runs on the embed.FS alone (no Postgres dependency, runs on every developer machine and every CI runner deterministically); TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase requires a real Postgres (creates a throwaway database via crypto/rand-named DSN, runs the embedded ladder, asserts schema_migrations is clean, skips with t.Skip when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset). The deterministic member is the FRONT LINE — it trips on every machine on any closed-set coverage regression (new NNNN_*.up.sql file the loader misses, numbered file renamed to non-numbered form, version gap). The Postgres member is the DEPTH gate — it asserts the runtime contract that no migration can fail on an empty DB. Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose contract spans deterministic-fixture-only AND requires-infrastructure invariants: BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety — deterministic Reversible check + Postgres Down test), BE-0392 (load smoke — deterministic harness contract + Postgres load run), BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — deterministic injection-point coverage + Postgres reconnect test). The Postgres member MUST use t.Skip on missing env var so the suite stays green on machines without Postgres — never t.Fatal, never silently pass. (2) `CLOSED-SET COVERAGE WALKS embed.FS, NOT RUNTIME.CALLER` — unlike BE-0388 (which read its own source via runtime.Caller to enumerate FuzzXxx targets), BE-0389's closed-set coverage walks the embedded migrations directory via fs.ReadDir(embeddedMigrations, migrationsDir) and parses each filename against a regex that MIRRORS the loader's grammar (`^(\d{4,})_([a-z0-9_]+)\.(up|down)\.sql$`). The matcher then cross-references against LoadMigrations(embeddedMigrations) to assert closed-set parity (same count, same versions in order, same names, non-empty bodies, 64-char checksums). The duplication of the filename grammar is INTENTIONAL — a future grammar change in the loader (e.g. permissive `.sql` form) MUST update both the loader AND the test in the same edit. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set surface is a directory tree (BE-0390 down-script coverage, BE-0398 tenant-isolation test coverage per repository method, BE-0407 desired-state goldens). (3) `SUBSTRING SET INCLUDES BOTH "schema_migrations" AND "NNNN_"` — the matcher binds to TWO load-bearing literals the migrations-from-empty-DB contract pivots on: `schema_migrations` (the ledger table — every applied migration MUST surface as one clean row) and `NNNN_` (the canonical filename grammar — closed-set coverage pivots on the four-digit-zero-padded version prefix). The substring set also includes `YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL` (the deterministic-skip env var) AND `YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY` (the shared opt-in-external escape hatch) so the matcher pins BOTH the per-suite skip contract and the cross-suite external contract. Substring count = 17 (matches BE-0388's count). The full FuzzXxx-style explicit-listing rule from BE-0388 carries over: when the matcher's substring set names the canonical function pair, the SECURITY.md section MUST quote each full function name verbatim — TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractCoversAllNumberedFiles AND TestMigrationsEmptyDBContractAppliesEmbeddedLadderToEmptyDatabase — so a reader sees the gate from the doc alone. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — NINE INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#15)` — inserting required step #15 between #14 (fuzz validator tests) and the optionals renumbered 15→16/16→17/17→18/18→19 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was EIGHT consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0388). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0388's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list canonical function pair verbatim), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: when the PRD's `-run` filter is NOT already in verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (BE-0389's `go test -run TestMigrationsEmptyDB ./...` was not), the implementation MUST add it via a Node fs round-trip (JSON.parse + JSON.stringify) to preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; direct text Edit can drift the formatting. Forward-applicable to BE-0390..BE-0399 sibling stories.
ralph/progress.txt:427:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fuzz-validators instance (BE-0388 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fuzz_validators_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/validate/fuzz_test.go's new canonical pair TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators + TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs + SECURITY.md "## Fuzz Validator Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fuzz tests for validators (BE-0388)" subsection; TENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0387 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestFuzzValidator ./...`, CI step name `Fuzz validator tests`, verify.sh prefix #14). Four BE-0388-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL PAIR WRAPS FuzzXxx TARGETS, NOT INDIVIDUAL VALIDATORS` — unlike BE-0386 (one fake, dual invariants) and BE-0387 (one redactor, dual invariants), BE-0388's load-bearing surface is a SET of nine FuzzXxx targets (FuzzName, FuzzPath, FuzzDomain, FuzzEnvVarName, FuzzEnvVarValue, FuzzDecodeJSON, FuzzImageRef, FuzzURL, FuzzGitBranch). The canonical-pair design splits the set into two orthogonal invariants: TestFuzzValidatorContractCoversExpectedValidators pins the CLOSED-SET COVERAGE — every public validator MUST have a FuzzXxx target, asserted by reading fuzz_test.go's own source via runtime.Caller and matching against `fuzzValidatorExpectedTargets` (the nine literal `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)` declarations). A new validator added to the public surface without a Fuzz target trips this matcher with the exact missing name. TestFuzzValidatorContractSeedCorpusRejectsHostileInputs pins the RUNTIME NO-PANIC invariant — the wrapper drives every validator through the shared `fuzzSeeds` corpus under a `recover()` guard so a panic surfaces with the offending validator name AND the seed index (the actionable-failure contract). Forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose load-bearing surface is a SET of targets sharing one invariant + a closed-set coverage rule: BE-0394 (chaos Postgres disconnects — set of disconnect injection points), BE-0395 (idempotency replay — set of idempotency-protected endpoints), BE-0398 (tenant isolation — set of customer-data queries). (2) `Fuzz-PREFIX TARGETS RUN UNDER -run TestFuzzValidator AND -fuzz=FuzzXxx` — Go's fuzz tests (signature `func FuzzXxx(f *testing.F)`) run their seed corpus deterministically when invoked with `go test -run FuzzXxx` AND drive the randomised engine when invoked with `go test -fuzz=FuzzXxx`. The BE-0388 design KEEPS the existing FuzzXxx targets unchanged (they continue to support the optional `-fuzz=` driver for soak runs) AND adds a TestFuzzValidator-prefixed wrapper pair that binds to the `-run TestFuzzValidator ./...` filter on every `go test ./...` invocation. The wrapper does NOT replace the FuzzXxx targets — it complements them by adding a deterministic, name-anchored Test* binding to the canonical filter. The randomised driver remains an opt-in escape hatch documented in SECURITY.md (`-fuzz=Fuzz<Name>`); CI runs the wrapper, soak runs drive `-fuzz=`. (3) `SUBSTRING SET ANCHORS THE STRING "no panic" VERBATIM, NOT HYPHENATED FORMS` — first CI run failed because the SECURITY.md text used "no-panic" (hyphenated, sentence-internal) while the matcher pinned "no panic" (space-separated, quoted). The fix wrapped the canonical phrase in quotes in the SECURITY section ("the wrapper pins the 'no panic' invariant") so both the matcher and the human-readable doc land on the same literal. Forward-applicable pattern: when pinning a multi-word invariant as a closed substring, prefer quoting the invariant in the doc so the matcher binds to the literal a reviewer is most likely to write. Also: when the matcher's substring set contains both bare ("FuzzName") AND prefix-form ("FuzzDecodeJSON") names, the section MUST list the full FuzzXxx target set explicitly — handing the reader a sample like "(`Name`, `Path`, ...)" doesn't satisfy a matcher that pins on the literal "FuzzName" substring. (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — EIGHT INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#14)` — inserting required step #14 between #13 (redaction tests) and the optionals renumbered 14→15/15→16/16→17/17→18 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The previous count was SEVEN consecutive insertions (BE-0380..BE-0387). Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0387's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 15-17-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline; list FULL target-name set explicitly when pinning a multi-target surface), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:428:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fake-Dokploy-contract instance (BE-0386 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fake_dokploy_contract_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go's new canonical pair TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs + TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials + SECURITY.md "## Fake Dokploy Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fake Dokploy contract tests (BE-0386)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384/BE-0385 verification-suite mega-pattern, confirms the BE-0385 closing-line prediction held verbatim — canonical command `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`, CI step name `Fake Dokploy contract tests`, verify.sh prefix #12). Four BE-0386-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL FILE = CONTRACT-OWNING PACKAGE, NOT A CONSUMER` — unlike BE-0385 which pinned `internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go` (the worker's lease test) and BE-0384 which pinned the quota test in the quota package, BE-0386's canonical file is `internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go` — the FAKE's own contract tests, not a downstream consumer's tests against the fake. The rationale: a fake-X contract-test story should bind to the package whose contract is being asserted (the fake itself), not to a caller that happens to use the fake. The worker test in `internal/controlplane/worker/dokployfake_test.go` (TestWorkerRunnerProvisionsThroughFakeDokploy, TestWorkerRunnerSurfacesFakeDokployFaults) is downstream evidence, NOT the canonical contract. Forward-applicable: any future verification story whose subject is a deterministic test fixture (BE-0392 load smoke harness, BE-0393 chaos test harness, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal) should pin the fixture's own contract tests, not a consumer's. (2) `-RUN FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD BUT MATCHED ZERO TESTS` — sibling of BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` but the OPPOSITE shape: the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...` before BE-0386 landed, BUT the `TestFakeDokploy` prefix matched ZERO existing tests anywhere in the repo (the dokployfake package's tests are TestCreateHierarchy…, TestTwoServers…, TestRequestRecordingRedactsCredentials, etc. — none start with TestFakeDokploy). The named CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING gates would have silently passed against zero tests. BE-0386 adds value by (a) CREATING the canonical TestFakeDokploy* pair so the `-run` filter binds to real assertions, (b) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces as named gates, (c) adding the static defence that pins both halves. Forward-applicable: when picking up ANY verification-suite story whose PRD `-run` filter exists, FIRST run `go test -run <Prefix> ./...` — if it shows `no tests to run` everywhere, the story MUST add canonical tests under the prefix BEFORE wiring the gates, otherwise the gates pass vacuously. (3) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — DETERMINISM × RECORDER-REDACTION` — TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs pins the per-server deterministic ID allocator (two independent dokployfake.New() servers driven through the same org/project/env/app/deployment chain mint byte-identical org_1/proj_1/env_1/app_1/dep_1, with per-server request counts isolated to 5/5); TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials pins the recorder hot path that swaps the Authorization header for output.Sentinel AND scans the body for the bearer literal (3 recorded requests across happy-path create / GET read-back / failure-path POST whose body deliberately echoes the bearer — each MUST have AuthHeader==Sentinel, Headers[Authorization]==Sentinel, no body retains the bearer literal). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `dokployfake.New` (constructor — deletion breaks every worker test), `Server.Requests` (recorder seam), `output.Sentinel` (the redaction substitute — a different sentinel would silently fork the redaction contract), AND `TestLiveDokploySmoke` (the AC2 opt-in-external escape hatch's test name) so auditors verify the contract from documentation alone. Substring count raised to 15 (vs BE-0385's 13). (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — SEVEN INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#12)` — inserting required step #12 between #11 (job worker lease) and the optionals renumbered 12→13/13→14/14→15/15→16 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). Now SEVEN consecutive required-step insertions with optional renumbering; the next contributor adding required step #13 (BE-0387 redaction tests, canonical `go test -run TestRedaction ./...` if PRD names that filter) should follow the same edit shape AND prepare for an optional-band renumber. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0385's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-15-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers (which seam each canonical-pair function pins). Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:429:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, job-worker-lease instance (BE-0385 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_job_worker_lease_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go's new canonical pair TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims + TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable + SECURITY.md "## Job Worker Lease Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: job worker lease tests (BE-0385)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0385-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER NEWLY ADDED TO PRD` — unlike BE-0383/BE-0384 where verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands already carried the `-run` filter, BE-0385's `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` was NOT present in the array before the story landed; the implementation adds it via a small JSON edit (preserve 2-space indent + trailing newline; use Node fs to round-trip parse/serialize so existing key order stays stable). (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — EXCLUSIVITY × SHUTDOWN-SAFETY` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestJobWorkerLeaseExclusivityNeverDoubleClaims pins the SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED claim in StoreClaimer.Claim (two concurrent goroutines race the same one-job queue; exactly one returns a non-nil Lease and the other returns (nil, nil); the surviving lease runs the job to success and the row carries no stale lease afterward); TestJobWorkerLeaseReleasedOnShutdownIsRetryable pins the Loop.Run shutdown path that invokes Lease.Release against a context detached from the cancelled loop context (a Loop.Run past a runner that blocks until cancellation, cancel the loop while the lease is in-flight, assert the persisted job is back to retrying with no stale lease owner and next_run_at claimable immediately). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (the claim) AND `Lease.Release` (the shutdown seam) so an auditor verifies both invariants from the documented contract alone. Forward-applicable to ANY future worker-verification story whose invariant has a "hot path concurrency × shutdown safety" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract tests can mirror this exclusivity × fault-injection pattern). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #11 between #10 (quota concurrency) and the optionals renumbered 11→12/12→13/13→14/14→15 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 11. Required: job worker lease tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern; this is now SIX consecutive required-step insertions (#6→#11) with optional renumbering, suggesting the next contributor adding a required step #12 (BE-0386 fake Dokploy) should follow the same edit shape: insert the new `# 12. Required` block AND renumber the four optional headers in the same Edit call. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0385 uses `go test -run TestJobWorkerLease ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0386 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0385: "Job worker lease tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (per-suite invariants; preserve the closed-set discipline so the matcher fails loudly), (e) AGENTS.md subsection (mirrors the SECURITY.md section but speaks to maintainers; explains the canonical-funcs pair invariants and which seam each pins).
ralph/progress.txt:430:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, quota-concurrency instance (BE-0384 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_quota_concurrency_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/quota/quota_test.go's new canonical pair TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates + TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation + SECURITY.md "## Quota Concurrency Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: quota concurrency tests (BE-0384)" subsection; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383 verification-suite mega-pattern). Three BE-0384-specific extensions to the template: (1) `RUN-FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD` — the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` before the story landed (same as BE-0383's policy-matrix entry; established by BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` sub-pattern). BE-0384 adds value via (a) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces (the gate didn't exist as a named gate before), (b) ADDING canonical TestQuotaConcurrency* tests (the existing TestCheckerReserveConcurrentRespectsLimit / TestCheckerReserveTenantIsolation siblings stay; the new pair encodes the FOR UPDATE-lock and organization_id-predicate invariants under the load-bearing prefix), (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. (2) `CANONICAL-PAIR INVARIANT-NAMING` — the canonical-funcs pair encodes the TWO load-bearing engine invariants: TestQuotaConcurrencyHardLimitNeverOverallocates pins the FOR UPDATE lock on the per-organization-per-resource usage counter row (parallel writers against ONE tenant — exactly limit succeed, the rest get yerr.CodeQuotaExceeded, the database row count agrees); TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation pins the organization_id predicate carried through every counter/sum/insert (parallel writers across TWO tenants — each respects its own limit AND the two tenants' active reservations sum to the two limits, never collapse into one bucket). The cross-tenant sum-check is the load-bearing defence-in-depth: per-tenant counts could pass while a regression silently dropped one tenant's rows entirely. The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `FOR UPDATE` (the lock) and `organization_id` (the predicate) so an auditor verifies the invariant from the documented contract alone, without opening the test file. Forward-applicable to ANY future concurrency-verification story whose invariant has a "single-tenant hot path × cross-tenant isolation path" dual: BE-0386 (fake Dokploy), BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). (3) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK CONTINUES` — inserting required step #10 between #9 (policy matrix) and the optionals renumbered 10→11/11→12/12→13/13→14 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). The static test pins `# 10. Required: quota concurrency tests` but NOT the optional headers — those shift on every required insertion. Established by BE-0380's renumber pattern. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0384 uses `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0385 will use `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0384: "Quota concurrency tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber trailing optionals in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope / determinism / actionable-failures (engine-specific tokens: FOR UPDATE, organization_id, CodeQuotaExceeded) / opt-in-external / envelope-pinning / canonical-pair / redaction / CI-cadence / self-locating gate filename, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry pre-existing OR newly inserted, (f) canonical reference test file path + canonical-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. GOTCHA: in TestQuotaConcurrencyTenantIsolation's per-org goroutine closure, do NOT alias `t` (the *testing.T) to a per-tenant tally bucket — `t := results[orgID]` SHADOWS the outer testing.T, silently breaking `t.Errorf` inside the closure. Used `bucket := results[orgID]` to keep the testing.T accessible for actionable diagnostics. Forward-applicable to any verification-suite story whose parallel-goroutine test groups results per-loop-var.
ralph/progress.txt:431:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, policy-matrix instance (BE-0383 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go's TestPolicyMatrix + the NEW sibling TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant + SECURITY.md "## Policy Matrix Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: policy matrix tests (BE-0383)" subsection; FIFTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern). Three BE-0383-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER WITHOUT PACKAGE NARROWING` — unlike BE-0381/BE-0382 which used `go test ./internal/controlplane/<pkg>/...` (package-narrowed) as the canonical command, BE-0383 uses the PRD's `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` form unchanged (filter-narrowed across the entire module). The CI step, verify.sh step, CONTRIBUTING entry, SECURITY row, and PRD entry ALL agree on the exact same `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` literal — there's NO package-narrowed variant. This keeps the gate stable across hypothetical future packages that may declare a TestPolicyMatrix… test (e.g. an httpapi-layer policy-matrix integration test). The static test's canonical-command constant is the single command echoed verbatim across all five external surfaces. (2) `CROSS-TENANT MATRIX TWIN` — the canonical-funcs list is a PAIR (TestPolicyMatrix + TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant), not a triple (BE-0382) or a single (hypothetical), because the policy engine has TWO load-bearing matrix invariants: in-tenant decisions (Decide's main path: ReasonAllowedSelf / ReasonAllowedByRole / ReasonDeniedNoCapability per `builtinRoleCaps[role].has(required)`) and cross-tenant decisions (Decide's cross-tenant clause: ReasonAllowedSelf for CapSelf, ReasonAllowedBySupport for support's CapSupport bridging CapRead/CapSupport only, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant for everything else). The two matrices are STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL (same role × action cross-product, same engine, same fixtures) but differ in resource scope (resourceIn(orgA) vs resourceIn(orgB)) and in the expected-verdict switch. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant has a "happy path × foreign-scope path" dual: BE-0398 (tenant isolation), BE-0404 (break-glass), BE-0407 (service desired-state goldens). The cross-tenant variant's switch MUST encode the engine's exact cross-tenant clause — guessing it from intuition (e.g. assuming Support is always allowed cross-tenant) fails the matrix because the engine ALSO checks the required cap via `roleCaps.has(CapSupport) && (required == CapRead || required == CapSupport)`. Verify by tracing engine.Decide BEFORE writing the test's expected-verdict switch. (3) `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` — unlike BE-0382 where the `-run TestOpenAPI` filter previously matched zero tests (the existing openapi tests were `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…`), BE-0383's `-run TestPolicyMatrix` filter ALREADY matched the existing `TestPolicyMatrix` function in internal/controlplane/policy/policy_test.go. The story still adds value by (a) wiring the dedicated CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY surfaces (the gate did not exist as a named gate before), (b) enriching the canonical test set with the cross-tenant matrix twin so the canonical-funcs pair is load-bearing, and (c) pinning the existence of both via the static test. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical command (BE-0383 uses `go test -run TestPolicyMatrix ./...` filter-narrowed; BE-0384 will use `go test -run TestQuotaConcurrency ./...`), (b) dedicated CI step name (BE-0383: "Policy matrix tests"), (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (BE-0383 inserted `# 9. Required: policy matrix tests` between BE-0382's `# 8` and the previous `# 9. Optional: govulncheck`, renumbering optionals 9→10/10→11/11→12/12→13 in the same edit — established by BE-0380's renumber pattern), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-entry substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/policy) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant) / exceptions (CapSelf, CapSupport) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / matrix-pair (TestPolicyMatrix, TestPolicyMatrixDeniesCrossTenant) / redaction (redacted) / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename (verification_suite_policy_matrix_static_test.go), (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry already present unchanged (BE-0383 did NOT add — the existing entry satisfies the matcher), (f) canonical reference test file path + matrix-pair func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:432:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, OpenAPI-conformance instance (BE-0382 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_openapi_conformance_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/openapi/openapi_conformance_test.go + SECURITY.md "## OpenAPI Schema Conformance Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: OpenAPI schema conformance tests (BE-0382)" subsection; FOURTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern). Two BE-0382-specific extensions to the template: (1) `-RUN-FILTER PREFIX CONTRACT` — when the PRD already carries a `-run TestXxx ./...` filter for the suite (BE-0382 inherited `-run TestOpenAPI ./...` from the PRD's verificationLoop array unchanged; that filter previously matched zero tests because the existing openapi tests are `TestBuild…` / `TestOperation…` not `TestOpenAPI…`), the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy the `-run` filter prefix or the named gate silently matches zero tests. The static test pins this with a dedicated `<subject>PrefixLocksTheRunFilterContract` analyzer subtest that walks the canonical-funcs list and asserts every entry begins with `func TestOpenAPI` (or whatever the suite's filter prefix is). Forward-applicable to BE-0383 (`-run TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (`-run TestQuotaConcurrency`), and BE-0386 (`-run TestFakeDokploy`); NOT applicable to BE-0385 (no `-run` filter — package-level only). When picking up such a sibling story FIRST check whether the PRD's `-run` filter currently matches any test (`go test -run <PrefixPattern> ./...` shows `no tests to run` everywhere) — if so, the story MUST add tests that satisfy the prefix, not just wire the dedicated step. (2) `VALUE-TYPE STRUCT FIELD SHAPE` — when the canonical conformance test reads a struct field that's a value type (not a pointer), the test MUST NOT guard with `field == nil` (compile error against the value type); the empty-struct sentinel is `field.SubField == ""` instead. This is a per-package shape detail; the canonical fix is to read the type once at write time, not to introduce a pointer indirection or a package-internal nil sentinel that does not exist today. Worked instance: openapi.Parameter.Schema is a `Schema` value not `*Schema`, so the conformance test checks `param.Schema.Type != "string"` directly. The six surfaces are: (a) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `OpenAPI schema conformance tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...`, (b) scripts/verify.sh `# 8. Required: OpenAPI schema conformance tests` header + literal command (BE-0381's prior steps 8/9/10/11 — govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser — renumbered to 9/10/11/12 in the same edit), (c) CONTRIBUTING.md `8. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (d) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## OpenAPI Schema Conformance Tests` section with a 13-entry closed substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/openapi) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (operationId) / authorization (x-required-action) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / conformance-triple (TestOpenAPIConformance, TestOpenAPIEnvelopesReferenceStableSchemaVersions, TestOpenAPIExamplesAreRedacted) / redaction / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename, (e) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry inserted immediately after the httpapi entry (so the two adjacent commands are the two package-level subsets that follow the umbrella `./internal/controlplane/...` walker), (f) canonical reference conformance test file `internal/controlplane/openapi/openapi_conformance_test.go` + load-bearing conformance triple covering (i) end-to-end document conformance (3.1.0 identity, both response rows per operation, public empty security, authenticated ApiKeyAuth + x-required-action, required-string-in-path parameters), (ii) envelope schema_version enum pinning to `output.SuccessSchema` / `yerr.SchemaVersion`, (iii) example redaction via `output.Sentinel` with negative assertion against the credential-shaped `yalla_sk_<env>_<value>` pattern; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteOpenAPIConformanceAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with 11 synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber the trailing optional steps in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 11-13 substring set covering every AC, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry (insert near siblings in the array; keep the umbrella entry FIRST), (f) canonical reference test file path + closed conformance-triple func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:433:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:434:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, HTTP-handler-contract-tests instance (BE-0381 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_handler_contract_tests_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## HTTP Handler Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: HTTP handler contract tests (BE-0381)" subsection; THIRD worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern: the static-test file pins SIX surfaces in one place — (1) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `HTTP handler contract tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`, (2) scripts/verify.sh `# 7. Required: HTTP handler contract tests` header + literal command, (3) CONTRIBUTING.md `7. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (4) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## HTTP Handler Contract Tests` section with a closed substring set covering scope/determinism/actionable-failures/contract-triple/envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1)/redaction/CI-cadence, (5) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry, (6) the canonical reference contract test file `internal/controlplane/httpapi/me_contract_test.go` AND its load-bearing contract triple (TestMeServerWritesResponseDataOnlyToResponseWriter, TestMeRequestLogRedactsBearerToken, TestMeErrorEnvelopeDoesNotLeakDependencyCause) so deleting the canonical example or renaming any of the three silently breaks the convention every other *_contract_test.go follows; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteHandlerContractAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures so over-tightening and under-tightening are both caught at the package-internal API; REUSES the shared constants requiredCIWorkflowPath, requiredVerifyShPath, requiredContributingPath, requiredContributingSection, requiredSecurityPath, requiredPRDPath defined in verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go; when inserting a new numbered verify.sh step, ALSO renumber subsequent optional steps in the same edit because the numeric prefix is part of the contract shared between verify.sh and the static-test constants — skipping the renumber breaks the BE-0380 contract-prefix matcher).
ralph/progress.txt:435:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, unit-tests-for-every-package instance (BE-0379 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go + internal/credentials/keyring_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Unit Test Suite" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: unit tests for every package (BE-0379)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the verification-suite single-file template called out in the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose subject is the suite itself rather than a specific security control). NINE load-bearing rules: (1) SINGLE FILE pins ALL surfaces of the meta-contract so a future contributor changing the CI step name, the verify.sh step header, the CONTRIBUTING numeric prefix, the SECURITY row, or the PRD verification-loop array fails ONE test, not five. The single-file shape is the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern carried forward to the verification-suite family. (2) CLOSED EXEMPT SET WITH RATIONALE — every exemption in allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests MUST carry a rationale that points to where coverage actually lives, not just a "thin entrypoint" hand-wave. The rationale is what makes a future review decide whether to keep, extend, or remove the entry; without it the set drifts. Current exempt set: cmd/yalla (Cobra entrypoint, behaviour in internal/cli), cmd/yalla-api (process wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/*), cmd/yalla-worker (loop wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/worker), internal/controlplane/jobs (doc.go placeholder). (3) STALE-EXEMPTION GUARD (TestVerificationSuiteExemptionSetIsTight) — assert every exempt dir (a) exists and (b) still lacks tests. Otherwise a package that gains tests silently keeps its exemption and the discipline rots. (4) IGNORED-PREFIX WALKER — the package coverage walker MUST skip `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `outputs/`, `tmp/`, `bin/`, `vendor/` via prefix-with-trailing-slash check (the trailing slash is what prevents a hypothetical `output/` package from being accidentally skipped). (5) PURE FUNCTION SPLIT — findPackagesMissingTests takes (root, exempt, ignored) and is called once by the runtime test against the real repo AND multiple times by the self-check with synthetic trees built via t.TempDir + mkFile helper. The self-check is what proves the walker doesn't false-positive on test-only dirs and doesn't false-negative on ignored subtrees. (6) CASE-SENSITIVE SUBSTRING CONSTANTS — `requiredSecuritySectionSubstrings` are matched verbatim against the doc, so a substring like "every Go package" will NOT match a sentence-start "Every Go package". When the doc text naturally starts a sentence with the substring, use the capitalised form in the constant. The first run caught this: the test failed with `missing required substring "every Go package"`; the fix aligned the constant with the doc, not the doc with the constant. The self-check fixture (synthetic SECURITY.md inside TestVerificationSuiteUnitTestsAnalyzerDetectsRegressions) must be updated in the same edit — if the synthetic fixture starts to fail, the test diagnostic explicitly says "fix the fixture, not the test" to prevent the wrong direction of fix. (7) NON-MAIN, NON-DOC-ONLY PACKAGE WITHOUT TESTS IS NEVER EXEMPT-WORTHY — prefer adding 2-3 small tests (e.g. internal/credentials IsNotFound + IsUnsupported + Store-interface compile-time pin) over expanding the exemption set. The exemption set is reserved for thin main-glue and `doc.go`-only placeholders. Adding the keyring_test.go tests took ~70 lines and was strictly preferable to a `internal/credentials` exemption entry, because the package has real classification logic (errors.Is over upstream keyring sentinels) that a future upstream rename could silently break. (8) PRD AS A VERIFICATION SURFACE — the prd.json's `verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands` array is itself part of the contract. An AI agent reads the PRD before picking up a story; pinning the suite command there guarantees the agent sees it without needing to discover CI workflows or shell scripts. The matcher uses encoding/json with a partial struct (`{VerificationLoop struct{RequiredLocalCommands []string `json:"requiredLocalCommands"`}}`) so a future PRD schema extension doesn't break it. (9) CI MATRIX AS PART OF THE CONTRACT — pinning ubuntu-latest + macos-latest + windows-latest is what prevents a silent "we only test on Linux now" regression. The matrix dimension is a separate substring assertion per host so the failure diagnostic names the missing host explicitly. Forward-applicable to BE-0380 (repository integration tests — replace findPackagesMissingTests with findStoreTestsMissingTenantIsolation), BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests — replace with findHandlersMissingContractTest), BE-0382 (OpenAPI conformance — replace with findEndpointsMissingOpenAPI), BE-0383 (policy matrix), BE-0384 (quota concurrency), BE-0385 (job worker leases), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade), BE-0391 (race detector), and every future P=1 verification-suite story. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical suite command (e.g. `go test -run TestMigrations ./...`), (b) scope predicate (which packages/files the matcher walks), (c) doc heading constant, (d) substring-set capturing the ACs of that story, (e) per-story exemption set if any. The pattern is the next-strongest form of the BE-0354 (dependency_scan) single-file multi-surface pattern, applied to a meta-contract whose subject is the test suite itself rather than a specific security control. The two pattern families share: (i) single file, (ii) closed substring/constant set per surface, (iii) self-check with synthetic fixtures driving every matcher. They differ in: (i) BE-0379-family stories have a `findPackagesMissingTests`-style repo walker as their primary matcher (the security-control family has no such walker; their primary matcher is an AST scan over package source); (ii) BE-0379-family stories pin the PRD itself (security-control stories generally don't); (iii) BE-0379-family stories use a closed exempt set with per-entry rationale (security-control stories use closed constant lists like `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments` but no per-entry rationale).
ralph/progress.txt:436:- Security-verification cross-package secret-confinement pattern, Dokploy-token instance (BE-0361 pattern, internal/release/dokploy_token_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/dokploy_token_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Dokploy Token Isolation" section + internal/controlplane/dokploy/AGENTS.md "Token isolation (BE-0361)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST cross-package instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346) and "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF) to "this scarce in-process secret MUST flow from source-of-truth package A through redaction package B into wire-emission package C with NO leak surface in customer-facing package D". Six load-bearing rules: (1) TRAILING-SELECTOR-NAME matching — when the field name is unambiguously the secret in the codebase ("DokployToken" is unique to the Config secret field, no other struct has a field of the same name), match by `SelectorExpr.Sel.Name == "DokployToken"` regardless of the receiver. This catches `config.DokployToken`, `cfg.DokployToken`, `c.DokployToken`, and any future-receiver shape alike, in ONE matcher rule, where the package-qualified-only variant would miss the `c.DokployToken` form a method using a `*config.Config` receiver would naturally write. The receiver-name freedom is the load-bearing rule: any closed list of qualifiers (`config.`, `cfg.`, …) is brittle to local-naming choices in a future refactor. (2) RETURN-FALSE-FROM-INSPECT to suppress double-counting when a matcher fires on a parent node whose children would ALSO match — `config.EnvDokployToken` is both a SelectorExpr (X=`config`, Sel=`EnvDokployToken`) and contains an inner `EnvDokployToken` Ident that the bare-Ident matcher would also flag. Returning `false` from the `ast.Inspect` callback after emitting the SelectorExpr diagnostic stops descent and keeps the count at 1. The self-check fixture `handler that reads config.EnvDokployToken is rejected` pins this — without the `return false` it would fail with `got 2, want 1`. (3) COUNT-AND-PIN-LOCATION instead of count-only — for invariants where the secret MUST appear at exactly one structural location (here, `c.token` in `client.go`'s Authorization header), the matcher returns the full set of `file:line:col` triples across every production file in the package, the test asserts `len == 1` AND the single hit's path-prefix equals the canonical file. A count-only assertion would let a future refactor move the line to a different file silently; a path-only assertion would let a duplicate slip in. The two assertions in series are strictly stronger than either alone. The constructor's `token: token` binding (a `KeyValueExpr.Key`, not a `SelectorExpr`) is NOT counted by construction — `ast.Inspect` walks the Key as an `Ident`, not a `SelectorExpr`, so the matcher's `SelectorExpr` pivot skips it for free. The self-check fixture `constructor binding token: token is NOT counted` pins this distinction. (4) COMPOSITE-LITERAL-FIELD-VALUE inspection for redaction-seam invariants — `(*Config).Redacted()` returns a `RedactedConfig{...}` composite literal whose `DokployToken` field value MUST be a CallExpr to the local `redact(...)` closure. The matcher uses `findMethodOnReceiver(file, "Config", "Redacted")` (a small helper that walks `file.Decls` filtering `FuncDecl.Recv.List[0].Type` against both `*ast.Ident` and `*ast.StarExpr` receivers), then walks the method body looking for ANY composite literal containing a `DokployToken` KeyValueExpr, then asserts the value is a CallExpr with `Fun.Name == "redact"`. Three orthogonal regressions fire one diagnostic each: missing method, missing field in the literal, bare-selector value instead of a call. The same pattern applies to ANY future "method M MUST stamp field F through helper H before returning" structural rule. (5) USES-FIRST gate for defence-in-depth log surfaces — `(*Config).LogValue()` MUST call `c.Redacted()` first AND MUST NOT touch `c.DokployToken` directly. The matcher walks the method body, tracking `seenRedactedCall` (set when a `c.Redacted()` CallExpr is found) and emitting a diagnostic for EVERY `c.DokployToken` SelectorExpr seen. The two diagnostics are independent — a method that called `c.Redacted()` but ALSO read `c.DokployToken` directly would fire one; a method that omitted the call but had no direct read would fire the other; a method that did both would fire both. Forward-applicable to any "MUST consult helper H before reading secret S" pattern (variables LogValue, future credentials redactor, etc.). (6) MARKER-PATTERN runtime gate at the projection seams — three table-driven tests in `dokploy_token_isolation_test.go` install a fixed `BE0361DOKPLOYTOKENMARKERXYZ` prefix on the Config.DokployToken field and assert the marker never appears in (a) the `json.Marshal(c.Redacted())` byte stream, (b) the `slog.JSONHandler` output under `slog.Any("config", c)`, and (c) the `c.String()` debug projection — for EACH of three fixtures: short-token (canonical), long-token (longer than the redactor's sentinel, longer than any reasonable line-buffered slog chunk so a partial-flush leak would still surface), and collision-prefix (token prefix collides with the projection's field name `dokploy_token`, so a regression that truncated at the field-name boundary would still trip the marker check). The marker is the leak detector — asserting on token bytes alone would false-positive whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the stable projection text. (7) GOLANGCI-LINT S1025 gotcha — staticcheck (under golangci-lint v2) flags `fmt.Sprintf("%s", x)` as redundant when `x` has a `String()` method, suggesting `x.String()` instead. For BE-0361 the intent of the runtime test was to PROVE that `fmt.Sprintf("%s", cfg)` doesn't leak, but since `fmt.Sprintf` with `%s` against a Stringer DELEGATES to `.String()`, calling `.String()` directly exercises the same code path and satisfies the linter. The test comment must explicitly document the equivalence so a future reader understands why the seemingly indirect "%s" check is actually identical to the direct Stringer invocation. The same gotcha applies to any future Stringer-surface security test.
ralph/progress.txt:437:- Security-verification package-confinement pattern, backup-encryption instance (BE-0362 pattern, internal/release/backup_encryption_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/encryption_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Backup Encryption" section + SECURITY.md Required Verification Gates table row + internal/controlplane/backup/AGENTS.md "Backup encryption boundary (BE-0362)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST whole-package-confinement instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346), "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF), and "this cross-package secret MUST flow through one seam" (BE-0361 Dokploy token) to "this leaf package MUST NOT carry an entire data plane". Eight load-bearing rules: (1) IMPORT-WHITELIST-AS-FORBIDDEN-LIST — the backup package's only legitimate I/O is os.ReadFile on the operator-supplied status path, so the gate enumerates the symmetric/asymmetric cipher set (crypto/aes, crypto/cipher, crypto/des, crypto/rc4, crypto/rsa, crypto/ecdsa, crypto/ed25519, crypto/tls), the archive/compression set (archive/zip, archive/tar, compress/gzip, compress/zlib, compress/flate, compress/bzip2), the database driver set (database/sql, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool), and the process-boundary set (os/exec, net/http) as a closed forbidden map. The set is intentionally aggressive because the boundary contract is "no data plane", not "this specific library is bad"; a future contributor who needs one of these libraries must surface the design change in review, not slip it in. (2) INTERFACE-METHOD-COUNT-AND-NAMES — the read-only Reporter interface is pinned by name AND by its single method's name (Status). The matcher locates the InterfaceType named Reporter, rejects every field in iface.Methods.List whose len(Names)==0 (embedded interfaces would let any method through, e.g. embed io.Writer would silently expand the surface), and rejects every named method whose Name != "Status". A missing interface fires a separate "missing required interface" diagnostic — a future refactor that renamed Reporter to BackupSurface would silently disable the gate otherwise. (3) CALL-EXPR-SELECTOR write-seam scan — the package MUST NOT call package-qualified write/exec functions. The matcher walks every CallExpr, asserts call.Fun is a SelectorExpr whose X is an ast.Ident (package alias) and joins pkg.Name + "." + sel.Sel.Name to compare against forbiddenBackupWriteSeams (os.Create, os.CreateTemp, os.WriteFile, os.OpenFile, os.Mkdir, os.MkdirAll, os.Rename, os.Remove, os.RemoveAll, os.Symlink, os.Link, os.Truncate, os.Chmod, os.Chown, exec.Command, exec.CommandContext). os.ReadFile / os.Stat / os.Open are deliberately excluded — they are the legitimate read-only seams. (4) MARKER-PATTERN runtime evidence with FOUR encryption-shaped fixtures — a parameterised subtest installs the BE0362KMSMARKERXYZ prefix inside four payload shapes (kms-key-id with arn:aws:kms:, aes-wrap-blob with base64-shaped repeated bytes, restore-failed-message with multi-line bucket URL, pem-private-key with BEGIN/END markers) and asserts the marker never appears in (a) err.Error(), (b) every errors.Unwrap layer (TestFileReporterEncryptionMarkerSurvivesErrorWrapping walks the wrap chain — a regression that wrapped raw content inside a deeper %w would be caught), or (c) json.Marshal(Status) (defensive: the struct has no content field today, but a future Detail field would surface the operator's accidentally-seeded payload through every downstream projection). (5) FIXTURE-SHAPE diversity for redaction robustness — every fixture exercises a different class of regression a future "improvement" to the parse-error message might introduce; the marker is what makes the leak detector load-bearing across all four shapes, because asserting on the fixture's full content would false-positive whenever the content happens to share a substring with a stable error phrase (e.g. a fixture containing "is malformed" would trip the existing "backup: status file is malformed" message). (6) WRAP-ROBUST DOC SUBSTRINGS — when pinning Markdown content from a wrapped document (SECURITY.md and the runbook), choose substrings short enough to survive line wrapping. The first iteration used "Plaintext WAL must never" and "no read access" — both spanned line wraps in the source files ("Plaintext WAL must\n   never" and "**no** read access") and the test fired false positives. Fix: use "Plaintext WAL" and "read access" — same semantic, robust to Markdown wrap and to **bold** runs. The closed substring list still names every load-bearing fact (KMS-managed key for at-rest, read access for the no-access invariant, Plaintext WAL for the WAL ban, Auditability for restore audit events) without being brittle to formatting. (7) DUAL-DOC PIN — the gate pins TWO operator-facing documents: SECURITY.md (the public posture) AND docs/operations/backup-restore.md (the operator runbook). Each gets its own test function with its own heading constant and closed substring set. Pinning both prevents a future "tidy" pass from collapsing one without the other — the operator-facing runbook is part of the public contract because operators rely on it to discover the KMS rotation cadence, the no-read-access invariant, and the audit-event posture. (8) SELF-CHECK with KNOWN-GOOD AND KNOWN-BAD pairs for EVERY matcher — TestBackupEncryptionStaticAnalyzerDetectsRegressions feeds three pairs (imports, reporter-interface, write-seams) through every matcher. Each known-bad fixture must produce ≥N hits (N matches the synthetic regression count); each known-good fixture must produce zero false positives. The reporter-interface self-check has a THIRD synthetic — a renamed interface (Surface) — that proves the matcher fires a "missing required interface" diagnostic when Reporter is removed, not just when its method set widens. A self-check that only feeds known-bad through the matcher cannot catch over-tightening (a matcher that fires on legitimate code would still satisfy the bad-fixture assertions); a self-check that only feeds known-good cannot catch under-tightening (a matcher that fires on nothing would still satisfy the good-fixture assertions). Both pairs are load-bearing. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is "this leaf package MUST NOT carry a data plane for cross-cutting concern X" — credential vaulting (the secrets package must not encrypt under a process-resident key), audit shipping (the audit package must not network out to a SIEM), rate-limit storage (the ratelimit package must not persist a token-bucket distributed store), backup restore orchestration (a future restore package's port shape), and KMS proxying (any future package that mediates KMS calls).
ralph/progress.txt:493:- Admin endpoint isolation static-pin pattern (BE-0357, internal/release/admin_endpoint_isolation_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Admin Endpoint Isolation" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "Admin endpoint isolation (BE-0357)" subsection; SECOND instance of the BE-0356 Go-source-file variant of the BE-0355 single-test-file template applied to ENTIRE-DIRECTORY-OF-SOURCE-FILES rather than a single file — structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is 'no production source under directory D may import package P, name selector S, or carry literal L'). Eight load-bearing rules: (1) The test file uses `package release_test` (NOT `package release`) so the canonical `projectRoot(t)` helper from release_test.go links — same gotcha as BE-0356, every static-pin test in internal/release MUST follow this convention. (2) The directory scan is bounded by a SINGLE small helper (`adminProductionFiles(t, root) -> []string`) that returns ONLY non-`_test.go` `.go` files plus the entry binary, sorted for deterministic diagnostics; test files are deliberately skipped because a future runtime regression test needs to mention the threat without tripping the gate. (3) The import scan parses with `parser.SkipObjectResolution|parser.ImportsOnly` (fast; ignores function bodies), the literal/selector scan parses with `parser.SkipObjectResolution|parser.ParseComments`; mixing modes by matcher is the right call when the matcher's needs differ. (4) Closed sets of forbidden imports (`net/http/pprof`, `expvar`, `golang.org/x/net/trace`), forbidden selector roots (`pprof`, `expvar`), and forbidden literals (`/debug/pprof`, `/debug/vars`, `/debug/requests`, `/debug/events`, `http.DefaultServeMux`) at the top of the test file — every set is closed, every member is named in SECURITY.md, every set extension is a deliberate two-file edit. (5) The http.Server `Handler` field is pinned by PRESENCE (omission falls back to DefaultServeMux per the stdlib docs) AND an explicit `Handler: nil` is rejected with the same diagnostic — both shapes are functionally equivalent and must both fail the gate. (6) The private-mux contract on `internal/controlplane/httpapi/server.go` is TWO orthogonal passes: a presence assertion (`http.NewServeMux` call must appear) AND a forbidden-reference scan (`DefaultServeMux` selector must not appear). Both are required — a regression that builds a private mux AND ALSO hands routes to the default mux would slip past either pass in isolation. (7) SECURITY.md gets a dedicated section heading (`## Admin Endpoint Isolation`), a verification-gates table row keyed on `-run TestAdminEndpointIsolation`, and a closed list of canonical substrings that name every forbidden import path BY NAME so a reader does not need to open the test file to learn the contract. (8) The runtime evidence half is implicit (the existing `notFoundRecorder` returns the stable `yalla.error.v1` envelope for any `/debug/*` request) — same single-test-file shape as BE-0355 and BE-0356. Forward-applicable to: any future security-verification story whose invariant is 'no production source file under directory D may carry shape X', including future stories for forbidden environment-variable echoes, forbidden log-format strings, forbidden cipher-suite literals, or forbidden file-scheme URL literals.
ralph/progress.txt:494:- TLS and proxy header trust static-pin pattern (BE-0356, internal/release/http_server_hardening_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## TLS Termination and Proxy Header Trust" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "TLS and proxy header trust" subsection; FIRST instance of the BE-0355 single-test-file template applied to GO SOURCE FILES instead of declarative manifests; structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant lives in (a) the construction shape of a typed struct literal in production Go code AND (b) the closed allow-list of string literals consulted in a designated function body). Six load-bearing rules: (1) The test file MUST use `package release_test` (NOT `package release`) so the canonical `projectRoot(t)` helper from release_test.go is in scope — the other static-pin tests in internal/release follow the same convention and a mismatched package gives an undefined-symbol build error at link time. (2) The struct-literal scan uses a package-qualified type selector helper (`isHTTPServerCompositeLit` matches a SelectorExpr with X=`http`, Sel=`Server`) so unrelated structs in the same source file are not scanned. Two closed sets ride on this matcher: `requiredHTTPServerFields = {ReadHeaderTimeout}` (every literal MUST include them, missing field = regression) and `forbiddenAPITLSStructFields = {TLSConfig, TLSNextProto}` (presence = regression). (3) The closed-set string-literal scan needs TWO ORTHOGONAL PASSES: a scoped pass on the named function body (`ClientIP` here — every `r.Header.Get(...)` literal must be in `allowedProxyHeaderLiterals` case-sensitively) AND a file-wide pass on every BasicLit in the file (no `forbiddenProxyHeaderLiterals` literal anywhere, case-insensitively). One pass is insufficient because a regression could land in a sibling helper added later; the file-wide pass catches that. Both passes share the same diagnostic prefix so operators see a consistent error format. (4) The scoped pass MUST also assert a required-identifier presence (`RemoteAddr` here — the fallback chain) AND emit a single 'missing function' hit if the named function is absent entirely, so the gate cannot pass silently when its target is removed by refactor. (5) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the test pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table (with the `-run <Prefix>` selector — use a single shared test-function name prefix like `TestHTTPServerHardening` so one selector covers all four tests) AND (b) a dedicated section heading AND (c) a closed list of canonical substrings (operator expectations, the closed allow-list of headers BY NAME so a reader cannot mistake the policy, the load-bearing field name like `ReadHeaderTimeout`, and the test file path itself as the link to the enforcement). Watch line breaks in SECURITY.md substrings — a typed prose paragraph reflows and the apostrophe in `operator's` can land on its own line, breaking `strings.Contains`; either rewrap to keep the canonical phrase on a single line (preferred) or split into shorter pinned substrings. (6) The self-check sub-test feeds known-good AND known-bad synthetic fixtures through every matcher — for the struct-literal scan, every required field AND every forbidden field AND every forbidden call selector gets its own isolated regression fixture, plus a multiple-regressions fixture that confirms hits compose additively (e.g. missing-field + forbidden-field + forbidden-call = 3 hits). Forward-applicable to: any future security-verification story whose invariant is 'this typed struct literal in production code MUST/MUST NOT carry field X' or 'this named function MUST consult only allow-listed string literals' (e.g. middleware composition order pins, context-key allow-lists, telemetry attribute redaction key allow-lists, future proxy-trust expansions for X-Forwarded-Proto or RFC 7239 `Forwarded` if Yalla ever adopts them).
ralph/progress.txt:495:- Container image hardening static-pin pattern (BE-0355, internal/release/container_hardening_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "Container Image Hardening" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "Container image hardening" subsection; structural template for ANY future security-verification story whose invariant lives in a non-Go declarative file — Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml, .github/workflows/*.yml, .goreleaser.yaml, Kubernetes manifests, OCI runtime spec). Single-test variant of the BE-0344 two-test pattern: the "runtime" half is implicit because the build/CI pipeline IS the runtime — there is no application-code call site to drive a parallel runtime test against, so the static analyser plus the self-check IS the load-bearing defence. Six load-bearing rules: (1) The parser lives in the test file itself, not in a separate package — keeps the production package API surface clean (only the SupportedTargets matrix in internal/release) and lets future hardening stories add their own parsers without coupling. The Dockerfile parser scope is intentionally narrow: join backslash continuations into logical instructions, resolve global ARG defaults so `FROM ${RUNTIME_IMAGE}` expands to the canonical value, group instructions under their owning FROM stage. Heredocs, `--mount=type=cache` flags, and ONBUILD are deliberately opaque — the matchers work above the parser's awareness of them. (2) Every invariant gets its own matcher function plus its own in-repo test plus its own self-check sub-test. The matcher takes the parsed model and returns a self-describing error; the in-repo test runs the matcher against the real Dockerfile/compose file; the self-check sub-test drives the matcher with a synthetic known-good baseline (a `minimalCanonicalDockerfile()` helper) AND a series of mutations each isolating one regression. (3) Required-value constants live at the top of the test file: `requiredRuntimeBaseImage = "gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot"`, `requiredNonrootUser = "nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredCopyChown = "--chown=nonroot:nonroot"`, `requiredBuilderBuildFlags = {CGO_ENABLED=0, -trimpath, -s -w}`. Closed sets for forbidden patterns: `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments = {TOKEN, PASSWORD, SECRET, API_KEY, PRIVATE_KEY, SIGNING_KEY, DSN}` (case-insensitive substring on ENV key), `forbiddenRuntimePackageInstalls = {apt-get, apt install, yum install, dnf install, apk add, microdnf install}` (substring on runtime stage's joined RUN payload). The closed-set design is deliberate — it catches the secret-shaped fragments the project's config layer actually uses, not every possible English word that contains "key", so a future ENV that holds a non-secret hint does not false-positive. (4) SECURITY.md is the human-facing co-contract: the same test file pins (a) a verification-gate row in the Required Verification Gates table AND (b) a dedicated `## Container Image Hardening` section with literal substrings (`distroless`, `nonroot`, `Multi-stage build`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, `-trimpath`, `Container image hardening`). A regression that drops either is caught as deterministically as a Dockerfile regression. (5) The postgres image pin regex `^postgres:\d+$` deliberately rejects variant tags like `postgres:16-bookworm` because the local integration-test stack must remain reproducible across local environments; if a future story needs a variant the regex must widen in lockstep with the fixture catalogue. (6) Builder stage vs runtime stage have ASYMMETRIC rules: builder stages may install packages (`apt-get install git` for the goreleaser-style git tag lookup is legitimate), the runtime stage may not. The matcher pins the asymmetry by walking only the runtime stage's instructions for the package-install scan and only the builder stage's instructions for the build-flag scan. The runtime stage is identified as the LAST stage (the unnamed final FROM); the builder stage as the FIRST stage with an `AS` name. Five test-fixture gotchas: (a) when SECURITY.md and the matcher disagree on case (`multi-stage` vs `Multi-stage`), the test pins the EXACT casing the documentation file uses — Go's `strings.Contains` is case-sensitive, and pinning the documentation casing makes the matcher serve as a stability anchor for the published policy. (b) Self-check cases that mutate ONE invariant on top of a shared canonical baseline are independent by construction — a future widening of matcher M cannot accidentally pass a fixture that was constructed to test matcher N. (c) The runtime stage's `COPY --from=builder` matcher MUST scope the `--chown=` requirement to COPY instructions that ALSO carry `--from=` — a plain `COPY ./foo /etc/foo` from the build context legitimately does not need the flag because the build context's owner is the current user. (d) The `ADD <url>` matcher uses a regex anchored at start-of-args that tolerates leading `--chown=` style flags; a future addition that allows local-path `ADD` would fail this assertion only on URL shapes. (e) The Dockerfile parser silently ignores instructions that appear before the FIRST FROM (other than ARG, which becomes a global ARG default). This is the Dockerfile spec behaviour; the test relies on it so `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` parser directives and pre-FROM comments don't accidentally count as instructions in any stage. NOT applicable to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract WITH an application-code call site (request body size, JSON parser hardening, log redaction fuzzing) — those follow the BE-0344 two-test (static + runtime) shape because the runtime test has a real handler/repository call site to drive.
