ralph/progress.txt:1036:- Dimensional (magnitude-axis) quota dimension on a Create unit of work (BE-0331 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/projectservice.go QuotaReserver port + internal/controlplane/quota/quota.go Checker + internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go serviceCPUMillicoresByKind + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_cpu_millicores_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0332 memory_mb / BE-0333 storage_gb / and every future quota dimension whose allocation is a numeric magnitude per request rather than a +1 count). The FIRST dimensional story widens the load-bearing seam ONCE for the entire dimensional-quota family: the store-layer QuotaReserver interface gains a second method `ReserveAmount(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string, amount int64) error`, and the real adapter quota.Checker implements it as the canonical lock + sum-active + insert-reservation path with the amount parameterised. The count-axis convenience `Reserve(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string) error` is refactored to a one-line delegate `return c.ReserveAmount(...,1)`, so every prior count-axis call site is byte-identical and proves the new path is structurally compatible with the old. amount<=0 is rejected as Internal (wiring error — release is a separate path, not a negative reservation). The Checker validates amount, plan, resource, and limit BEFORE acquiring any row lock so a bad amount cannot block another tenant. The unit-of-work call site (ServiceService.Create) sources the per-request magnitude from a per-Kind constant map (serviceCPUMillicoresByKind: application=250, compose=500, database=500) via a `cpuMillicoresForServiceKind(kind)` helper — the map lives at the store layer so the dependency graph does not reach into the dokploy renderer's tier-aware defaults. The ReserveAmount call runs in the SAME *Tx as the count-axis Reserve calls, the desired-state write, the job enqueue, and the audit append; ordering is "every count-axis Reserve first, then the dimensional ReserveAmount" so a tenant who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
ralph/progress.txt:1047:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, on-every-PR required-step-INSERTION instance (BE-0401 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_release_build_static_test.go + internal/release/release_build_test.go's new canonical pair TestReleaseBuildCoversCallSites + TestReleaseBuildPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Release Build Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table (with When = "Every push and PR") + CONTRIBUTING.md entry 25 + Step 25 paragraph + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: release build tests (BE-0401)" subsection; TWENTY-THIRD worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0400 verification-suite mega-pattern AND THE FIRST WORKED INSERTION OF A NEW REQUIRED VERIFY.SH STEP INTO THE MIDDLE OF THE SEQUENCE (every prior BE-0379..BE-0399 instance appended its required step at the trailing end of the required block; BE-0401 inserts at #25 BETWEEN BE-0399's #24 and the existing optional block). Canonical command `go test -run TestReleaseBuild ./...`. The TWO BE-0401-specific shifts from the on-every-PR template are forward-applicable to every future required-step insertion (not just verification stories): (1) `INSERTING A REQUIRED VERIFY.SH STEP AT POSITION #N FORCES A RENUMBER OF EVERY OPTIONAL STEP #N..end IN scripts/verify.sh AND A SYNCHRONISED CONSTANT UPDATE IN EVERY PRIOR STATIC TEST THAT PINNED AN AFFECTED STEP HEADER` — for BE-0401, the verify.sh optional steps #25..#29 (govulncheck, staticcheck, golangci-lint, goreleaser check, external smoke) renumbered to #26..#30, AND `verification_suite_external_dokploy_smoke_static_test.go` (BE-0400) had to update `externalDokploySmokeVerifyShStepHeader` from `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` to `# 30. Optional…` AND the two synthetic-fixture strings inside the analyser's `TestVerificationSuiteExternalDokploySmokeAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` self-check (the `# 28. Optional: goreleaser check` known-bad fixture to `# 29. Optional…` and the `# 29. Optional: external live-Dokploy smoke tests` known-good fixture to `# 30. Optional…`) in the SAME edit. The renumber cost is bounded — only static tests that pin step headers in the affected range need updates — but a missed update fails the analyser self-check, NOT silently. Forward-applicable to every future required-step insertion: grep all `# N. Optional` and `# N. Required` literals in `internal/release/verification_suite_*_static_test.go` for the renumbered range and update each in the same commit; the AGENTS.md subsection for the inserting story documents the renumber as the shared cost. (2) `PURE PROJECTION HELPERS (NO LIVE INFRASTRUCTURE) STILL KEEP THE CANONICAL PAIR SHAPE` — the release package's `ArchiveExt`/`BinaryName`/`ArchiveName`/`ChecksumsName` are pure functions today, so the contention-burst member of the canonical pair would seem redundant. But the burst is the load-bearing stability check the gate's wire contract requires AND keeping the pair shape consistent across every BE-0379..BE-0401 gate makes the static analyser's canonical-funcs matcher reusable and the operator-facing AGENTS.md narrative consistent. Forward-applicable: every future verification-suite gate (pure-function or stateful) keeps the closed-set-coverage + per-decision-stability pair shape so the `-run TestX` filter binds to a two-function fixture the static gate can name, even when the burst's race tripwire is the only load-bearing reason for the second function to exist.
ralph/progress.txt:1052:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED short-lived claim/ledger table whose ONLY repository mutation surface is an InsertReservation (one-shot active-row INSERT) plus a single resolver read (SumActiveReservations), with NO per-row Update/Delete/Get, NO version column, a settled_consistent CHECK pairing status<>'active' with NON-NULL settled_at, a CHECK amount>0, a composite-FK out (organization_id, job_id) -> provisioning_jobs added by a LATER migration than the row's own migration, and a database-managed bump-only set_updated_at trigger fired BEFORE UPDATE** (BE-0455 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0453 (quota_usage -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) and BE-0451 (quota_policies -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in six load-bearing ways: (a) the row-shape test must anchor BOTH a nullable-pointer JobID (zero on a no-JobID call, *string round-trip on a non-empty-JobID call) AND a nullable-pointer SettledAt (zero on every active-row insert -- there is no public surface that can mint a settled row); (b) the JobID round-trip leg cannot use a synthetic id alone because migration 0008 added a composite FK quota_reservations_job_fk -> provisioning_jobs (organization_id, id), so the test MUST pre-seed a real provisioning_jobs row (`INSERT INTO provisioning_jobs (id, organization_id, job_type, idempotency_key) VALUES (...)`) before InsertReservation -- a regression that dropped the FK or swapped its column order would surface here; (c) the settled_consistent CHECK violation has NO independent client-input surface (settled_at is not in the INSERT column list of InsertReservation), so the test exploits the OTHER direction: passing Status=ReservationStatusCommitted (non-blank, non-'active') sends settled_at=NULL via the default and the CHECK rejects it, proving InsertReservation can ONLY mint ACTIVE rows; (d) the time-filter leg of SumActiveReservations is a SECOND-AXIS exclusion test alongside the status filter -- a row with status='active' AND expires_at<=now must be excluded by the `expires_at > $3` predicate, so the test pre-seeds an active-but-time-expired row and asserts it is excluded WITHOUT mutating its status (a regression that dropped the time predicate would silently sum already-expired rows); (e) the resource-filter leg seeds TWO active reservations under the SAME tenant with DIFFERENT resources and asserts BOTH `SumActiveReservations(org, A, now)` and `SumActiveReservations(org, B, now)` return their own resource's amount (a regression that dropped the resource predicate would return 11 instead of 3 from either call); (f) the duplicate-ID Conflict test uses an explicit caller-supplied ID instead of relying on the qres_ prefix-mint path, because the PK violation is the constraint and a regression that silently re-minted the explicit id would mask the test (the explicit-ID-preservation test PRECEDES the duplicate-ID test and pins the no-remint contract). Forward-applicable to every future short-lived-claim repository whose public surface is a single one-shot active-row INSERT plus a resolver-read (a hypothetical lease-claim table KindResourceLease with active->held|released|expired, or a future job_attempts row whose public surface is a single INSERT per attempt). Test-helper naming caveat at package store_test: every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same package, so the type name `quotaReservationTxRollbackSentinel` must be distinct from quotaPolicyTxRollbackSentinel / quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel / projectTxRollbackSentinel and friends -- grep `TxRollbackSentinel` before adding a new sentinel. Helper REUSE strategy: this file DEFINES its own `runInsertReservationOrFail` / `loadQuotaReservationRowByID` / `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg` / `rawQuotaReservationRow` (no shared symbols to reuse), and REUSES the existing `insertReservationRow` / `insertSettledReservationRow` helpers defined in quota_repository_test.go for the SumActiveReservations setup legs because those raw-SQL inserts can stamp the schema's required `settled_at = now()` for non-active rows without going through the repository's active-only mint path.
ralph/progress.txt:1053:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED CLAIM-PROTOCOL TABLE WHOSE FOUR REPOSITORY SURFACES (Claim upsert with steal-expired branch / Complete state-transitioning UPDATE / Release state-conditioned DELETE / Find composite-tuple read) ALL CARRY organization_id AS THE LEADING COLUMN OF A COMPOSITE PREDICATE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES NULLABLE COMPLETION COLUMNS (response_status / response_body / completed_at) DISTINGUISHED BY NIL-NESS UNDER A COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK, AND WHOSE INVARIANTS FILE ALREADY LANDED IN THE IMMEDIATELY-PRIOR PRD STORY** (BE-0470 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_tenant_isolation_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0468 (audit_events) cross-tenant template with seven load-bearing deltas: (a) there is NO ListByOrganization read surface — Find is the only read, scoped to the full (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple — so the "cross-tenant list returns only own rows" probe collapses to "cross-tenant Find returns (zero, false, nil)" with a per-tenant count assertion on both sides, AND the "limit clamping is tenant-scoped" probe drops entirely (no list cap exists). The file's package-doc header MUST document the absence so a future reader does not look for a missing test, mirroring the BE-0426 organizations file's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" doc-only delegation; (b) the byte-identity comparator MUST handle three nullable pointer-bearing columns (*int for response_status, []byte for response_body, *time.Time for completed_at) where the pending-vs-completed lifecycle distinction is observable through nil-ness. A simple field-equality loop will silently round-trip a regression that flipped a pending row's NULL completion columns to zero-value scalars (e.g. response_status=0, response_body=[]byte{}, completed_at=epoch). The pattern: switch on (after==nil, baseline==nil) into three branches — both-nil OK, one-nil diff "nil-ness drifted", both-non-nil dereference-compare — and emit a fmt-printer-pair (fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr) so the diagnostic reads "before=<nil> after=200" rather than pointer noise. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file whose row carries nullable completion columns (e.g. job_attempts with nullable error_summary + finished_at + result, quota_reservations with nullable released_at + job_id); (c) every mutating surface needs its OWN OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB pair — Claim, Complete, Release. The Complete probe is structurally distinct from the Claim probe because Complete's WHERE composes `organization_id = $1 AND principal_id = $2 AND idempotency_key = $3 AND status = 'pending'` so the cross-tenant caller falls off the organization_id leg BEFORE the status leg runs, and the typed Conflict ("no longer pending") fires WITHOUT a foreign-row read — a regression that dropped organization_id from the Complete WHERE would either rewrite orgB's row (worst case) OR silently complete orgA's "claim" with a forged response envelope. The Release probe asserts the dual: Release is "safe to call defensively" and returns nil on a zero-row DELETE, so the cross-tenant Release must return nil AND leave orgB's pending row byte-identical AND the per-tenant count unchanged — three separate assertions because a regression that dropped organization_id from the Release WHERE would delete orgB's row, which the bystander byte-identity loader catches as a missing-row error inside loadIdempotencyRowByID's t.Fatalf. The forged Complete envelope MUST carry a recognisable canary string (`"forged":"orgA-tries-to-complete-orgB-claim"`) so a future reader can see immediately what the test was protecting against; (d) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe needs a GLOBAL row-count assertion in addition to the per-tenant counts. The PRIMARY KEY on idempotency_keys.id is global, so a rolled-back PK violation under a foreign tenant could in principle land the failing row in some other (third) tenant's slot — the per-tenant probe alone cannot prove the absence because the failing row's organization_id is unknown to the probe. The fix is a tiny totalIdempotencyRows(ctx, t, db) helper that runs `SELECT count(*) FROM idempotency_keys` with no WHERE, snapshotted before and after the failing Claim. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for a table whose PK is global (not parent-scoped); (e) the Claim cross-tenant id-collision probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) tuples so the ON CONFLICT target does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY violation flows through mapWriteError. A naive collision attempt under the same (principal, key) tuple would be intercepted by ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE and silently rewritten through the expiry-takeover branch — the test would pass on a broken implementation. The id-collision test in this file mints sharedID via mintIdempotencyID and assigns it to a record carrying a DIFFERENT (principal_id, idempotency_key) than the seeded orgA row. The pattern is forward-applicable to every future tenant-isolation file for an UPSERT table whose ON CONFLICT target is a NON-PK unique tuple (idempotency_keys uses (org, principal, key); job_attempts may use (job_id, attempt_number); etc.); (f) the Claim "mirror tuple" probe is the load-bearing per-tenant-uniqueness assertion of the ON CONFLICT target. orgB seeds a row at (orgB, usr_mirror, mirror-key); orgA Claims a row at (orgA, usr_mirror, mirror-key) — same (principal_id, idempotency_key), different organization_id. A regression that dropped organization_id from the ON CONFLICT target would resolve the conflict onto orgB's mirror-tuple row and silently rewrite it through the DO UPDATE branch — the worst-case cross-tenant idempotency data-corruption shape. The assertion: orgA's Claim returns owned=true (fresh INSERT, never DO UPDATE on a foreign row), orgA's new row carries orgA's organization_id and a distinct id, AND orgB's mirror row remains byte-identical (every field including the nullable completion columns and the database-assigned created_at + caller-supplied expires_at). The "mirror tuple" terminology and the explicit comment ("the load-bearing bystander whose presence on orgB exercises the per-tenant uniqueness of the ON CONFLICT target") MUST be carried verbatim in the seed comment because the structural test failure mode (silent peer-row rewrite) is the kind a future reader will rationalise away without it; (g) the cascade-from-organization-delete probe MUST seed orgB rows covering the lifecycle spread — pending, completed-with-envelope, pending-with-future-expiry — because the nullable completion columns are the load-bearing byte-identity surface, not the always-set columns. The single-bystander variant of this cascade is already pinned by the BE-0469 invariants file's TestIdempotencyRepositoryCascadeFromOrganizationDelete (one orgB row, inline column equalities); the tenant-isolation file deepens it with a three-row pool and the assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical comparator across every row, plus a post-cascade Find for every surviving orgB row to prove the cascade did not silently corrupt the repository read predicate. New helpers introduced: assertIdempotencyRowByteIdentical (nullable-aware comparator), fmtIntPtr / fmtTimePtr / itoaInt (pointer-friendly diagnostic formatters — itoaInt avoids importing strconv solely for the formatter call sites), seedTwoTenantIdempotencyFixture (two-organization shape; idempotency_keys has no project/environment/service parent chain so the fixture is narrower than the dokploy_refs equivalent), totalIdempotencyRows (global PK-collision count probe). Helpers reused: seedOrg (schema_test.go); newIdempotencyStore / idemFixture / claimIdem / completeIdem / findIdem / wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go); rawIdempotencyRow / loadIdempotencyRowByID / countIdempotencyRowsForOrg / mintIdempotencyID (idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go).
