ralph/progress.txt:27654:- Updated internal/release/AGENTS.md with a new `## Verification suite: unit tests for every package (BE-0379)` section explaining the SIX surfaces, the exemption-set discipline, the self-check shape, and the "update both ends in one edit" rule for future contributors.
ralph/progress.txt:27660:  - go test ./internal/release/... -run TestVerificationSuite -count=1 → PASS (7 test functions + 8 self-check subtests)
ralph/progress.txt:27665:  - Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test (BE-0379 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go; structural template for BE-0380..BE-0408 sibling verification-suite stories and any future "the suite itself is part of the contract" verification). NINE load-bearing rules: (1) ONE file pins ALL surfaces of the meta-contract so a future contributor changing the CI step name, the verify.sh step header, the CONTRIBUTING numeric prefix, the SECURITY row, or the PRD verification-loop array fails ONE test, not five. The single-file shape is the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern carried forward to the verification-suite family. (2) CLOSED exempt set with rationale — every exemption MUST carry a rationale that points to where coverage actually lives, not just a "thin entrypoint" hand-wave. The rationale is what makes a future review decide whether to keep, extend, or remove the entry; without it the set drifts. (3) STALE-exemption guard (TestVerificationSuiteExemptionSetIsTight) — assert every exempt dir (a) exists and (b) still lacks tests. Otherwise a package that gains tests silently keeps its exemption and the discipline rots. (4) IGNORED-prefix walker — the package coverage walker MUST skip `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `outputs/`, `tmp/`, `bin/`, `vendor/` via prefix-with-trailing-slash check so a hypothetical `output/` package (without the trailing s) is still scanned. (5) PURE function split — findPackagesMissingTests takes (root, exempt, ignored) and is called once by the runtime test against the real repo AND multiple times by the self-check with synthetic trees built via mkFile (t.TempDir + os.WriteFile). The self-check is what proves the walker doesn't false-positive on test-only dirs and doesn't false-negative on ignored subtrees. (6) CASE-SENSITIVE substring constants — `requiredSecuritySectionSubstrings` are matched verbatim against the doc, so a substring like "every Go package" will NOT match a sentence-start "Every Go package". When the doc text naturally starts a sentence with the substring, use the capitalised form in the constant. The first run caught this: the test failed with `missing required substring "every Go package"` and the fix was to align the constant with the doc, not the doc with the constant. The self-check fixture must also be updated in the same edit (the synthetic SECURITY.md fixture in TestVerificationSuiteUnitTestsAnalyzerDetectsRegressions). (7) NON-MAIN, NON-DOC-ONLY package without tests is NEVER exempt-worthy — prefer adding 2-3 small tests (e.g. `internal/credentials` IsNotFound + IsUnsupported + Store-interface compile-time pin) over expanding the exemption set. The exemption set is reserved for thin main-glue and `doc.go`-only placeholders. (8) PRD as a verification surface — the prd.json's `verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands` array is itself part of the contract. An AI agent reads the PRD before picking up a story; pinning the suite command there guarantees the agent sees it without needing to discover CI workflows or shell scripts. The matcher uses encoding/json with a partial struct so a future PRD schema extension doesn't break it. (9) CI MATRIX as part of the contract — pinning ubuntu-latest + macos-latest + windows-latest is what prevents a silent "we only test on Linux now" regression. The matrix dimension is a separate substring assertion per host so the failure diagnostic names the missing host explicitly.
